## ast-grep Community Best Practices

ast-grep rule writing and usage best practices. This skill should be used when writing, reviewing, or debugging ast-grep rules for code search, linting, and transformation. Triggers on tasks involving YAML rules, pattern syntax, meta variables, constraints, or code rewriting.

- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/SKILL.md`
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/AGENTS.md`: **Version 0.2.0** ast-grep Community January 2026
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/assets/templates/_template.md`: Brief explanation (1-3 sentences) of WHY this matters. Focus on the problem being solved and its impact.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/README.md`: Comprehensive best practices guide for ast-grep rule writing and usage, designed for AI agents and LLMs.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/compose-all-for-and-logic.md`: The `all` composite rule requires a node to satisfy every sub-rule. Use it to combine multiple conditions that must all be true.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/compose-any-for-or-logic.md`: The `any` composite rule matches if at least one sub-rule succeeds. Use it for alternative patterns that should trigger the same action.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/compose-field-targeting.md`: The `field` option in relational rules (`inside`, `has`) restricts matching to specific named children of AST nodes. Use it to distinguish between function bodies, parameters, conditions, and other structural elements.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/compose-has-for-children.md`: The `has` relational rule confirms that a matched node contains specific descendant nodes. Use it to filter parent nodes by their content.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/compose-inside-for-context.md`: The `inside` relational rule verifies that matched nodes appear within a specific parent structure. Use it to limit pattern matches to relevant contexts.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/compose-matches-for-reuse.md`: The `matches` rule references utility rules by ID. Define common patterns once and reuse them across multiple rules.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/compose-not-for-exclusion.md`: The `not` rule inverts matching logic. Use it to exclude specific patterns from broader matches.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/compose-precedes-follows.md`: The `precedes` and `follows` relational rules match nodes based on their order among siblings. Use them to find patterns that depend on statement sequence.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/const-kind-filter.md`: Constraints filter captured meta variables after pattern matching. Use `kind` constraints to ensure variables match expected node types.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/const-not-inside-not.md`: Constraints cannot be used inside `not` rules because `not` succeeds when nothing matches, leaving no node to constrain.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/const-pattern-constraint.md`: Use `pattern` constraints to verify that captured meta variables match a specific code structure, not just kind or text.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/const-post-match-timing.md`: Constraints filter results after pattern matching completes. A pattern must first match successfully before constraints are evaluated.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/const-regex-filter.md`: Use `regex` constraints to filter meta variables by their text content. The regex must match the entire node text.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/meta-multi-match-lazy.md`: The `$$$` multi-match operator is lazy, not greedy. It stops at the first valid match to ensure linear-time performance.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/meta-named-vs-unnamed.md`: Single `$VAR` only matches named AST nodes. Use `$$VAR` to match unnamed nodes like operators and punctuation.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/meta-naming-convention.md`: Meta variables must start with `$` followed by uppercase letters, underscores, or digits. Invalid names silently fail to capture.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/meta-reuse-binding.md`: When the same meta variable name appears multiple times in a pattern, all occurrences must match identical code. Use different names for independent captures.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/meta-single-node.md`: A single `$VAR` matches exactly one AST node. It cannot match multiple consecutive nodes like function arguments or statements.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/meta-underscore-noncapture.md`: Prefix meta variables with underscore (`$_`) when you don't need their captured value. This avoids HashMap creation during matching, improving performance.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/org-file-filtering.md`: Use `files` and `ignores` globs to apply rules only to relevant files. This reduces false positives and improves scan performance.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/org-message-clarity.md`: Write messages that explain what's wrong and how to fix it. Use `note` for detailed guidance and `labels` for precise highlighting.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/org-project-structure.md`: Organize ast-grep projects with standard directories for rules, utilities, and tests. The `sgconfig.yml` file defines project root and directories.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/org-severity-levels.md`: Set severity levels that reflect the actual impact of rule violations. This enables developers to prioritize fixes and configure CI appropriately.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/org-unique-rule-ids.md`: Rule IDs must be unique across the entire project. Use descriptive names that indicate the rule's purpose and enable targeted suppression.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/pattern-avoid-comments-strings.md`: ast-grep matches AST nodes, not text. Patterns will never match content inside comments or string literals because those are leaf nodes without children matching code structure.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/pattern-context-selector.md`: Code fragments like object keys or function parameters cannot be parsed standalone. Use `context` to provide surrounding structure and `selector` to target the specific node.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/pattern-debug-ast.md`: When patterns don't match expected code, use `--debug-query` to inspect the actual AST structure. Misunderstanding node types is the most common pattern failure.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/pattern-kind-vs-pattern.md`: Use `kind` for broad node-type matching and `pattern` for specific code structures. Combining them incorrectly causes unexpected results.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/pattern-language-aware.md`: Identical pattern strings parse differently across languages. Single quotes denote strings in JavaScript but character literals in C/Java.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/pattern-nthchild-matching.md`: The `nthChild` atomic rule matches nodes by their position among siblings. Use it to target specific elements in arrays, function parameters, or statement sequences.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/pattern-range-matching.md`: The `range` atomic rule matches nodes by their character position in the source file. Use it for precise location-based targeting when other methods are insufficient.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/pattern-strictness-levels.md`: The `strictness` parameter controls how precisely patterns must match AST structure. Looser settings match more variations but risk false positives.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/pattern-valid-syntax.md`: Patterns must be syntactically valid code that tree-sitter can parse. Invalid patterns silently fail to match anything, wasting debugging time.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/perf-avoid-regex-heavy.md`: Regex matching is slower than AST pattern matching. Use patterns and kind filters first, then apply regex only when necessary.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/perf-specific-patterns.md`: More specific patterns match fewer nodes, improving scan performance. Avoid overly generic patterns that match most of the codebase.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/perf-stopby-boundaries.md`: Relational rules like `inside` and `has` search the entire tree by default. Use `stopBy` to limit search depth and improve performance.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/perf-thread-parallelism.md`: ast-grep scans files in parallel by default. Tune thread count for optimal performance on your hardware and codebase size.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/rewrite-meta-variable-reference.md`: The `fix` template replaces matched code textually. Any captured meta variable not referenced in the fix is lost.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/rewrite-preserve-semantics.md`: Rewrites replace matched code textually. Ensure the replacement maintains identical behavior to avoid introducing subtle bugs.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/rewrite-syntax-validity.md`: Fix templates are inserted textually without parsing. Ensure meta variable substitutions produce syntactically valid code for all possible matches.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/rewrite-test-before-deploy.md`: Always test rewrites with `--interactive` mode on representative samples before running on entire codebase. Edge cases in real code often break assumptions.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/rewrite-transform-operations.md`: The `transform` section modifies captured meta variables before inserting into the fix. Use it for case conversion, substring extraction, and regex replacement.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/test-edge-cases.md`: Include edge cases that stress pattern boundaries. Real codebases contain unusual but valid code that breaks naive patterns.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/test-playground-first.md`: Use the ast-grep playground for rapid pattern iteration. The interactive environment shows matches immediately and displays AST structure.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/test-snapshot-updates.md`: Snapshot testing captures expected rewrite output. Use `--update-all` to regenerate snapshots when intentionally changing fixes.
- `.claude/skills/ast-grep/references/test-valid-invalid-cases.md`: Test files must include both `valid` (should not match) and `invalid` (should match) cases. This verifies both precision and recall.

