- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/forensic-enf-analysis.md`: Electric Network Frequency (ENF) from power grid hum embeds a unique signature. Compare with historical ENF databases to verify when and where a recording was made.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/forensic-metadata.md`: Audio files contain metadata about recording device, date, and processing history. Essential for establishing provenance and detecting manipulation.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/forensic-speaker-id.md`: Extract voice features (pitch, formants, speaking rate) for speaker comparison. Can help identify or exclude individuals.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/forensic-tampering.md`: Audio can be edited to add, remove, or rearrange content. Forensic analysis detects discontinuities in spectral, temporal, and ENF patterns.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/noise-adaptive-estimation.md`: Static noise profiles fail when noise changes over time (traffic, crowd, wind). Adaptive algorithms continuously update their noise estimate.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/noise-avoid-overprocessing.md`: Aggressive noise reduction creates "musical noise" (twinkling artifacts) and removes speech harmonics. Use the minimum reduction that achieves intelligibility.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/noise-identify-type.md`: Different noise types require different algorithms. Stationary noise (hum, hiss) responds to spectral subtraction; transient noise (clicks, pops) needs time-domain repair.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/noise-profile-silence.md`: Noise reduction algorithms need a pure noise sample. Extract from segments where no speech occurs for accurate spectral estimation.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/noise-snr-assessment.md`: Without SNR measurement, you cannot verify improvement. Always quantify before/after to ensure processing helped rather than harmed.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/signal-analyze-first.md`: Blind enhancement can worsen audio. Always analyze the recording first to identify noise types, frequency content, and clipping before selecting processing strategy.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/signal-bit-depth.md`: Each arithmetic operation on low bit-depth audio accumulates quantization errors. Process in 32-bit float to preserve precision across multiple operations.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/signal-lossless-format.md`: Each lossy encoding cycle (MP3, AAC, OGG) permanently removes audio information. Use WAV or FLAC for all intermediate processing steps.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/signal-preserve-original.md`: Always work on copies of audio evidence. The original recording is legally irreplaceable and any modification—even "enhancement"—can render it inadmissible in court.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/signal-sample-rate.md`: Resampling introduces interpolation errors. Process at the original sample rate; only resample for final delivery requirements.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/spectral-band-limiting.md`: Human speech occupies 80 Hz to 8 kHz. Filtering outside this range removes noise without affecting intelligibility. Telephone-band (300-3400 Hz) often sufficient for recognition.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/spectral-declip.md`: Clipped audio has flattened peaks that create harmonic distortion. Declipping algorithms reconstruct the original waveform shape using interpolation or sparse reconstruction.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/spectral-equalization.md`: Targeted EQ boosts speech frequencies while cutting noise bands. Focus on presence (2-4 kHz) for consonant clarity and cut mud (200-400 Hz) for definition.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/spectral-notch-filter.md`: Electrical hum (50/60 Hz) and its harmonics appear as sharp spectral lines. Notch filters surgically remove these without affecting surrounding frequencies.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/spectral-subtraction.md`: Spectral subtraction removes noise by subtracting the estimated noise spectrum from each frame. Effective for consistent background noise (HVAC, hiss, fan).
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/spectral-wiener-filter.md`: The Wiener filter provides mathematically optimal noise reduction by minimizing mean squared error. Superior to basic spectral subtraction for varying SNR.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/temporal-dynamic-range.md`: Compression reduces the gap between quiet and loud parts, making soft speech audible without clipping loud segments.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/temporal-noise-gate.md`: Noise gates mute audio below a threshold, eliminating background noise during pauses while preserving speech.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/temporal-silence-trim.md`: Remove leading/trailing silence and normalize levels for consistent playback. Essential for transcription workflows and evidence presentation.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/temporal-time-stretch.md`: Slowing down speech (without pitch change) gives listeners more time to parse difficult audio. Critical for degraded recordings with high cognitive load.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/temporal-transient-repair.md`: Transient damage (clicks, pops, digital dropouts) obscures speech. Repair by detecting anomalies and interpolating from surrounding audio.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/tool-audacity-workflow.md`: Audacity provides visual waveform/spectrogram editing essential for manual forensic work that automated tools can't handle.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/tool-batch-automation.md`: Batch processing ensures consistent enhancement across multiple recordings. Use shell scripts and Python for reproducible workflows.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/tool-ffmpeg-essentials.md`: FFmpeg is the Swiss Army knife of audio processing. Master these commands for efficient forensic workflows.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/tool-install-guide.md`: Complete installation guide for audio forensic tools on macOS, Linux, and Windows.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/tool-python-pipeline.md`: Python with librosa, soundfile, and scipy enables custom analysis and processing pipelines for forensic workflows.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/tool-quality-assessment.md`: Objective quality metrics validate that enhancement improved the audio. Measure before and after processing.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/tool-sox-commands.md`: SoX (Sound eXchange) excels at precise audio manipulation, especially noise reduction with profiles and effects chaining.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/transcribe-confidence.md`: ASR confidence scores identify words that need human review. Flag low-confidence sections for manual verification.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/transcribe-hallucination.md`: Whisper can hallucinate repeated phrases, URLs, or random text during silence. Implement detection and filtering.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/transcribe-multipass.md`: Single transcription pass may miss words or hallucinate. Multiple passes with different settings or models enables consensus verification.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/transcribe-segment.md`: Long audio with silence causes ASR hallucinations. Segment into speech chunks for better accuracy and timestamps.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/transcribe-whisper.md`: OpenAI's Whisper is trained on diverse, noisy data making it more robust than traditional ASR for degraded audio.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/voice-dereverb.md`: Reverb (room echo) smears speech and reduces intelligibility. Dereverberation algorithms suppress late reflections while preserving direct sound.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/voice-dialogue-isolate.md`: When noise includes other voices, music, or complex sounds, source separation AI models can isolate the target speaker more effectively than traditional filtering.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/voice-enhance-speech.md`: Cloud AI services (Adobe Enhance, Descript) provide studio-quality enhancement without technical expertise. Useful for quick processing when forensic documentation isn't required.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/voice-formant-preserve.md`: Formants (resonant frequencies) define voice character. Pitch shifting without formant preservation creates unnatural "chipmunk" or "Darth Vader" effects.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/voice-frequency-boost.md`: Different phonemes occupy different frequency ranges. Target boosting based on what's missing to recover specific sounds.
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/voice-rnnoise.md`: RNNoise uses a recurrent neural network trained on diverse noise types. Superior to traditional methods for complex, non-stationary noise (crowds, traffic, wind).
- `.claude/skills/audio-voice-recovery/references/voice-vad-segment.md`: Voice Activity Detection (VAD) identifies speech vs. silence/noise segments. Apply aggressive processing only to non-speech segments to preserve speech quality.

