## Chrome Extensions UX/UI Best Practices

Chrome Extensions UX/UI design and implementation guidelines for popups, side panels, content scripts, and options pages. Triggers on tasks involving browser extension UI, manifest v3, chrome APIs.

- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/SKILL.md`
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/AGENTS.md`: **Version 0.1.0** Chrome Extensions Community January 2026
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/assets/templates/_template.md`: Brief explanation (1-3 sentences) of WHY this matters for Chrome extension UX/UI. Focus on user impact and development implications.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/access-aria-labels.md`: Provide text alternatives for buttons that only display icons. Screen readers announce nothing useful for unlabeled icon buttons.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/access-color-contrast.md`: Maintain minimum contrast ratios between text and background colors. Low contrast text is unreadable for users with visual impairments and difficult for everyone in bright conditions.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/access-focus-trap.md`: Ensure keyboard users can always navigate out of any component. A focus trap occurs when Tab/Shift+Tab cycling never escapes an element, leaving users stuck.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/access-focus-visible.md`: Never remove focus outlines without providing an alternative. Keyboard users must see which element is currently focused to navigate your extension.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/access-keyboard-navigation.md`: Ensure every interactive element is reachable and operable via keyboard. Users who cannot use a mouse, and power users who prefer keyboard, must access all features.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/access-semantic-html.md`: Use native HTML elements (`<button>`, `<input>`, `<select>`) instead of styled `<div>` or `<span>` elements for interactive components. Native elements provide keyboard support and screen reader announcements automatically.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/brand-badge-text.md`: Limit badge text to 4 characters maximum. Longer text gets clipped and becomes unreadable in the small badge space.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/brand-consistent-styling.md`: Apply consistent colors, typography, and spacing across all extension surfaces (popup, side panel, options, injected UI). Inconsistent styling looks unprofessional and confuses users.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/brand-distinctive-icon.md`: Create a unique, recognizable icon that stands out in the browser toolbar. Generic icons blend into the crowd, making your extension hard to find.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/brand-icon-sizes.md`: Supply icons in all required sizes (16, 32, 48, 128px) to ensure crisp display everywhere. Missing sizes cause Chrome to scale existing icons, resulting in blurry appearance.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/brand-web-store-assets.md`: Prepare high-quality screenshots and promotional images for the Chrome Web Store. Poor listing assets dramatically reduce install conversion rates.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/comp-action-tooltip.md`: Set a clear, action-oriented tooltip for your extension icon. The default shows only the extension name, which doesn't tell users what clicking will do.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/comp-content-script-ui.md`: Inject UI directly into web pages when users need to interact with page content. Forcing users to switch between page and popup/panel creates friction and breaks flow.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/comp-minimal-permissions.md`: Request only the permissions your extension absolutely needs. Excessive permissions trigger browser warnings that scare users away during installation.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/comp-popup-vs-sidepanel.md`: Use a side panel instead of a popup when users need to reference extension content while browsing. Popups close automatically when users click outside, forcing repeated re-opening for ongoing tasks.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/comp-single-purpose.md`: Build extensions around one narrowly defined purpose. Chrome Web Store requires extensions to have a single, easily understood function. Multi-purpose extensions face rejection and user confusion.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/feedback-badge-status.md`: Use the action badge to show important status without requiring users to open the extension. Keep badge text short (4 characters max visible).
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/feedback-error-messages.md`: Provide specific, actionable error messages that tell users what happened and how to fix it. Generic errors leave users confused and helpless.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/feedback-loading-states.md`: Display immediate feedback when operations take more than 100ms. Users perceive unresponsive UI as broken; loading indicators set expectations.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/feedback-notifications.md`: Reserve system notifications for truly important events. Overusing notifications trains users to ignore them or disable your extension entirely.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/feedback-progress-indication.md`: Display progress indicators for operations taking more than 2 seconds. Show either percentage complete or steps remaining so users know the operation is progressing.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/feedback-success-confirmation.md`: Provide clear confirmation when actions complete successfully. Silent success leaves users uncertain whether their action worked.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/inject-cleanup.md`: Remove all injected elements and event listeners when the extension is disabled or the UI is dismissed. Orphaned elements cause memory leaks and interfere with pages.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/inject-document-ready.md`: Wait for the DOM to be ready before injecting UI elements. Injecting too early causes errors when target elements don't exist yet.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/inject-shadow-dom.md`: Wrap injected UI in Shadow DOM to prevent style leakage. Without isolation, your styles affect the page and page styles break your UI.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/inject-unique-ids.md`: Prefix all injected element IDs with a unique namespace. Generic IDs like "container" or "modal" will conflict with page elements.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/inject-z-index.md`: Set z-index to the maximum value (2147483647) for injected overlays. Many sites use high z-index values that can hide your extension's UI.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/options-auto-save.md`: Save settings immediately when users change them. Requiring manual save leads to lost changes and adds unnecessary friction.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/options-embedded-page.md`: Use embedded options pages for extensions with few settings. Embedded pages display inline on the extensions management page, avoiding unnecessary tab switches.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/options-sensible-defaults.md`: Ship with carefully chosen defaults so the extension works immediately. Users should only visit settings to customize, not to make the extension functional.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/options-sync-storage.md`: Use `chrome.storage.sync` for user preferences so settings follow users across their devices. Local-only storage forces users to reconfigure on each computer.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/panel-lazy-sections.md`: Load only visible panel sections initially. Defer loading of collapsed or below-the-fold sections until users need them.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/panel-non-distracting.md`: Design side panels to enhance browsing without competing for attention. Panels that distract from page content get closed quickly and rarely reopened.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/panel-page-context.md`: Update side panel content to reflect the current page when relevant. A panel showing stale or unrelated content feels disconnected and useless.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/panel-responsive-width.md`: Build side panel layouts that adapt to user-resized widths. Users can drag the panel edge to resize; fixed-width designs break when resized.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/panel-tab-vs-window.md`: Decide whether your side panel should be tab-specific (different content per tab) or window-wide (same content across all tabs). The wrong choice confuses users.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/popup-auto-close.md`: Save user input continuously because popups close instantly when users click outside. Never require explicit save actions for in-progress work.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/popup-dynamic-switch.md`: Use `chrome.action.setPopup()` to display different popups based on application state. Show users only the UI relevant to their current context.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/popup-external-js.md`: Place all JavaScript in separate .js files linked via `<script src>`. Manifest V3 prohibits inline scripts (`onclick`, `<script>` tags with code) due to Content Security Policy requirements.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/popup-instant-render.md`: Display meaningful content immediately when the popup opens. Users perceive delays over 100ms as laggy. Fetch data after rendering the initial UI.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/popup-primary-action.md`: Design popups with a single, visually prominent primary action. Users open popups with intent—make the main action impossible to miss.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension-ui/references/popup-size-constraints.md`: Design popup content to fit within Chrome's enforced size constraints (min 25×25px, max 800×600px). Content exceeding these limits gets clipped or requires scrolling that users don't expect.

## Chrome Extension Best Practices

Chrome Extensions (Manifest V3) performance and code quality guidelines. Use when writing, reviewing, or refactoring Chrome extension code including service workers, content scripts, message passing, storage APIs, TypeScript patterns, and testing.

- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/SKILL.md`
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/AGENTS.md`: **Version 0.1.0** Chrome Developer Relations January 2026
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/assets/templates/_template.md`: Brief explanation of WHY this matters (1-3 sentences). Focus on the performance implications and user impact.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/README.md`: A comprehensive performance optimization guide for Chrome Extensions (Manifest V3), designed for AI agents and LLMs.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/api-avoid-redundant-api-calls.md`: Calling Chrome APIs inside loops creates unnecessary overhead. Fetch data once before the loop, or batch operations where the API supports it.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/api-handle-context-invalidated.md`: When an extension is updated or reloaded, existing content scripts become orphaned. Their `chrome.*` API calls will throw "Extension context invalidated" errors. Handle this gracefully.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/api-query-tabs-efficiently.md`: Querying all tabs and filtering in JavaScript wastes resources. Use `chrome.tabs.query` filter options to let the browser return only relevant tabs.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/api-use-alarms-minperiod.md`: The chrome.alarms API enforces a minimum period of 1 minute. Shorter values are silently rounded up, which can cause unexpected behavior if you're expecting sub-minute intervals.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/api-use-declarative-content.md`: Instead of waking the service worker on every navigation to check if your icon should be enabled, use `declarativeContent` to let the browser handle it natively.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/api-use-promises-over-callbacks.md`: Modern Chrome Extension APIs support promises. Promise-based calls are cleaner, support async/await, and make error handling easier with try/catch.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/comp-adapter-interface.md`: Define adapter interfaces for communication between extension contexts. This decouples UI components from background implementation.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/comp-content-script-structure.md`: Organize content scripts with clear initialization, message handling, and cleanup phases. Always handle the extension context invalidation.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/comp-css-class-patterns.md`: Use BEM-inspired class naming with double underscores for elements and double dashes for modifiers.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/comp-manager-class.md`: Use classes with static methods for singleton managers in background scripts. Initialize with a static `init()` method.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/comp-type-guards.md`: Create type guard functions for validating unknown data at runtime. Name them with `is` prefix and use `x is T` return type.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/comp-ui-components.md`: Structure UI components with explicit props interface, typed state, and consistent export patterns.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/content-batch-dom-operations.md`: Reading layout properties and modifying the DOM in alternating sequence causes layout thrashing. Batch all reads together, then all writes, to minimize expensive browser reflow calculations.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/content-minimize-script-size.md`: Content script JavaScript must be parsed and compiled on every page load. Unlike web pages, extension scripts don't benefit from HTTP cache for compilation. Large bundles significantly slow down every page the user visits.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/content-programmatic-injection.md`: Manifest-declared content scripts run on every matching page load, even if unused. Programmatic injection using `chrome.scripting.executeScript` loads scripts only when the user actually needs the feature.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/content-use-document-idle.md`: The default `document_idle` timing injects scripts after the DOM is ready but before all resources load. Using `document_start` blocks the page while your script runs. Only use earlier injection when absolutely necessary.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/content-use-mutation-observer.md`: Polling the DOM with setInterval wastes CPU cycles checking for changes that haven't happened. MutationObserver is event-driven and only runs when the DOM actually changes.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/content-use-specific-matches.md`: Using `<all_urls>` or overly broad match patterns injects your content script into every page, slowing down all browsing and increasing memory usage. Specify the exact domains and paths your extension needs.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/err-context-invalidation.md`: Handle "Extension context invalidated" errors gracefully in content scripts. This error occurs when the extension updates or reloads while a content script is still running on a page.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/err-early-return.md`: Use early returns for guard clauses to avoid deep nesting and make the "happy path" clear.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/err-null-coalescing.md`: Use nullish coalescing (`??`) and optional chaining (`?.`) for accessing potentially undefined values. This is safer than `||` which treats `0`, `''`, and `false` as falsy.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/err-promise-barrier.md`: Use the PromiseBarrier pattern for coordinating async operations that need to wait for initialization to complete. This prevents race conditions during extension startup.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/err-storage-operations.md`: Wrap storage operations in try-catch blocks. Storage can be unavailable in private browsing, when quota is exceeded, or when the user has disabled storage.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/err-url-parsing.md`: Always wrap URL parsing in try-catch and return null or a sensible default on failure. User URLs, tab URLs, and external URLs can all be malformed.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/err-validation-pattern.md`: Validation functions should return an object with errors array and corrected values, not throw exceptions. This allows callers to decide how to handle errors and enables partial recovery.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/mem-avoid-closure-leaks.md`: Closures capture their enclosing scope. If a long-lived callback references a large object from an outer scope, that object stays in memory for the lifetime of the callback.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/mem-avoid-detached-dom.md`: When a DOM element is removed from the page but your JavaScript still references it, the element (and its entire subtree) cannot be garbage collected. This is a common source of memory leaks in content scripts.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/mem-cleanup-event-listeners.md`: Event listeners attached to page elements persist even after your content script context is destroyed. These orphaned listeners accumulate memory and can cause unexpected behavior.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/mem-clear-intervals-timeouts.md`: Uncleaned intervals continue running after content script context is destroyed, causing errors and wasted CPU. Always clear timers when your script unloads.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/mem-use-weak-collections.md`: When caching data associated with DOM elements, use `WeakMap` instead of `Map`. WeakMap allows elements to be garbage collected when removed from the page, preventing memory leaks.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/msg-avoid-broadcast-to-all-tabs.md`: Sending messages to all tabs wastes resources when only specific tabs need the update. Query for relevant tabs and target messages specifically.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/msg-check-lasterror.md`: Chrome sets `chrome.runtime.lastError` when API calls fail (e.g., tab closed, SW not ready). Failing to check it causes silent failures and uncaught error warnings in the console.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/msg-debounce-frequent-events.md`: Events like scroll, resize, and mousemove fire dozens of times per second. Sending a message for each event overwhelms the message channel and wastes CPU on redundant processing.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/msg-minimize-payload-size.md`: Messages between extension contexts are JSON-serialized. Large payloads increase serialization time and memory usage. Send only the data needed, not entire objects or DOM snapshots.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/msg-use-ports-for-frequent.md`: **Incorrect (new connection per message):**
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/net-limit-csp-modifications.md`: Stripping or weakening Content Security Policy headers breaks site security and can cause functionality issues. If you must modify CSP, do so surgically for specific use cases.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/net-request-minimal-permissions.md`: Over-requesting permissions is the #1 rejection reason in Chrome Web Store review. Request only what your extension actually needs, and prefer `activeTab` over broad host permissions.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/net-use-activetab.md`: The `activeTab` permission grants temporary access to the current tab when the user clicks your extension icon. It shows no install warning and works on any site without requesting `<all_urls>`.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/net-use-declarativenetrequest.md`: The `webRequest` API intercepts every request in JavaScript, adding latency. `declarativeNetRequest` uses browser-native rule matching that's significantly faster and doesn't require a persistent background page.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/storage-avoid-storing-large-blobs.md`: **Incorrect (base64-encoded images in storage):**
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/storage-batch-operations.md`: Each `chrome.storage` call has async overhead. Reading or writing multiple values in separate calls multiplies this overhead. Use object syntax to batch operations.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/storage-cache-frequently-accessed.md`: Reading from `chrome.storage` on every access adds async overhead. For values read frequently (like feature flags or user settings), cache them in memory and update via storage change listeners.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/storage-choose-correct-type.md`: Using `storage.sync` for large data hits quota limits and throttling. Using `storage.local` for cross-device settings creates inconsistent user experience. Match storage type to data characteristics.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/storage-use-session-for-temp.md`: **Incorrect (temporary data in persistent storage):**
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/style-boolean-naming.md`: Prefix boolean variables with `is`, `has`, `was`, `did`, `should`, or `can` to make their boolean nature explicit.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/style-cache-naming.md`: Suffix cache variables and Maps with `Cache` to indicate their caching purpose.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/style-constants.md`: Use SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE for module-level constants and static readonly class properties.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/style-directory-structure.md`: Organize code by browser extension context rather than by technical layer. This maps directly to how browser extensions work: background scripts, content scripts (inject), and UI (popup/options).
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/style-file-naming.md`: Use kebab-case (lowercase with hyphens) for all file names. This ensures consistency and avoids case-sensitivity issues across different operating systems.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/style-function-naming.md`: Use camelCase for functions with consistent prefixes that indicate the function's purpose.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/style-import-type.md`: Use `import type` for imports that are only used for type annotations. This ensures they are removed during compilation and don't affect bundle size.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/style-index-entry-points.md`: Use `index.ts` as the main entry point for each feature directory to control the public API and simplify imports.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/style-message-enums.md`: Name message type enums with direction indicator `MessageType[Source]to[Target]` and use kebab-case values with direction prefix.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/style-type-naming.md`: Use PascalCase for types and interfaces without prefixes. Use descriptive suffixes for common patterns.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/sw-avoid-keepalive.md`: Service workers are designed to be ephemeral. Keeping them alive unnecessarily consumes memory and CPU, negating Manifest V3's efficiency benefits. Design for event-driven wake-ups instead.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/sw-persist-state-storage.md`: Service workers terminate after 30 seconds of inactivity. Any global variables are lost when this happens. Use `chrome.storage.local` or `chrome.storage.session` to persist state across service worker restarts.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/sw-register-listeners-toplevel.md`: Event listeners must be registered synchronously at the top level of your service worker. If listeners are registered inside async callbacks or conditionally, events may be missed when the service worker wakes up.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/sw-return-true-async.md`: When using `sendResponse` asynchronously in a message listener, you must `return true` to keep the message channel open. Otherwise, the channel closes immediately and the sender receives `undefined`.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/sw-use-alarms-api.md`: **Incorrect (timer lost when SW terminates):**
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/sw-use-offscreen-for-dom.md`: Service workers have no DOM access. For operations requiring `DOMParser`, `canvas`, clipboard, or audio playback, create an offscreen document instead of injecting content scripts into arbitrary pages.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/test-browser-api-mocking.md`: Create mock implementations of browser extension APIs for unit testing. This allows testing background and content script logic without a browser.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/test-organization.md`: Place tests in a `tests/` directory mirroring the source structure. Use `.tests.ts` suffix for test files.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/test-validation-functions.md`: Validation functions should be thoroughly tested with edge cases, invalid inputs, and boundary conditions. Use table-driven tests for comprehensive coverage.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/ui-batch-badge-updates.md`: Rapidly updating the browser action badge (text and color) causes visual flicker and unnecessary API calls. Debounce badge updates and set both properties in a single logical update.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/ui-minimize-popup-bundle.md`: Popup HTML, CSS, and JavaScript are loaded fresh each time the popup opens. Large bundles cause noticeable delays. Keep popup bundles minimal and defer heavy operations.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/ui-render-with-cached-data.md`: Users expect popups to open instantly. Waiting for fresh data before rendering causes perceived lag. Render immediately with cached data, then update when fresh data arrives.
- `.claude/skills/chrome-extension/references/ui-use-options-page-lazy.md`: Options pages with many settings and complex UI can take time to render. Load only the visible section initially, then lazy load other tabs/sections when accessed.

