## Clean Architecture Best Practices

Clean Architecture principles and best practices from Robert C. Martin's book. This skill should be used when designing software systems, reviewing code structure, or refactoring applications to achieve better separation of concerns. Triggers on tasks involving layers, boundaries, dependency dire...

- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/SKILL.md`
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/assets/templates/_template.md`: Brief explanation (1-3 sentences) of WHY this matters for software architecture. Focus on the architectural implications and cascade effects.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/adapt-anti-corruption-layer.md`: When integrating with external systems that have different models, build an anti-corruption layer (ACL) that translates between their model and yours. Never let external concepts pollute your domain.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/adapt-controller-thin.md`: Controllers should only handle HTTP concerns: parsing requests, validating input format, calling use cases, and formatting responses. No business logic.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/adapt-gateway-abstraction.md`: Database gateways, API gateways, and service gateways are polymorphic interfaces that hide external system details. The use case layer talks to abstractions; infrastructure implements them.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/adapt-mapper-translation.md`: Mappers translate between domain entities and external representations (database rows, API responses, DTOs). Each layer has its own model; mappers bridge them.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/adapt-presenter-formats.md`: Presenters accept data from use cases and format it for presentation. They create view models with strings, booleans, and pre-formatted values - nothing left for the view to compute.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/bound-boundary-cost-awareness.md`: Boundaries are expensive to create and maintain, but ignoring needed boundaries becomes very expensive later. Continuously evaluate where the cost of implementing is less than the cost of ignoring.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/bound-defer-decisions.md`: A good architecture allows major decisions about frameworks, databases, and delivery mechanisms to be deferred until the last responsible moment. The longer you wait, the more information you have.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/bound-humble-object.md`: The Humble Object pattern separates hard-to-test behaviors from easy-to-test behaviors. The "humble" part contains minimal logic and is hard to test; the substantial logic goes in a testable component.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/bound-main-component.md`: The Main component is the lowest-level, dirtiest component. It creates all factories, strategies, and global facilities, then hands control to high-level abstractions. Treat it as a plugin that can be swapped.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/bound-partial-boundaries.md`: When you anticipate needing a boundary but the cost of full separation is too high, implement a partial boundary. This prepares for future separation without premature over-engineering.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/bound-service-internal-architecture.md`: Breaking a monolith into microservices doesn't solve architectural problems. Each service still needs internal architecture. Services are a deployment option, not an architecture.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/comp-common-closure.md`: Classes that change for the same reasons and at the same times should be in the same component. This is the Common Closure Principle (CCP) - the Single Responsibility Principle for components.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/comp-common-reuse.md`: Classes used together should be in the same component. Classes not used together should be in separate components. Forcing a client to depend on things it doesn't use creates unnecessary coupling.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/comp-reuse-release-equivalence.md`: Classes grouped into a component should be releasable together. A component should have a version number, release notes, and clear documentation. This is the Reuse/Release Equivalence Principle (REP).
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/comp-screaming-architecture.md`: The folder structure should communicate what the system does, not what framework it uses. Looking at the top-level directories should reveal the business domain.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/comp-stable-dependencies.md`: Components should depend on components that are more stable than themselves. A stable component (many dependents, few dependencies) should not depend on an unstable component (few dependents, many dependencies).
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/dep-acyclic-dependencies.md`: The dependency graph must be a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG). Cycles create ripple effects where changes propagate unpredictably through the system.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/dep-data-crossing-boundaries.md`: Data crossing architectural boundaries should be simple, isolated data structures. Never pass entities, database rows, or framework objects across boundaries.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/dep-interface-ownership.md`: Interfaces should be defined in the layer that uses them, not the layer that implements them. The client owns the abstraction; the implementation adapts to it.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/dep-inward-only.md`: The Dependency Rule states that source code dependencies can only point inward toward higher-level policies. Inner circles must never reference outer circles.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/dep-no-framework-imports.md`: Entities and use cases must never import framework-specific types. Framework dependencies in inner layers create tight coupling that makes testing slow and migration impossible.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/dep-stable-abstractions.md`: The most flexible systems depend on abstractions, not concretions. Volatile concrete classes are under active development and change frequently; depending on them propagates instability.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/entity-encapsulate-invariants.md`: Business rules that must always be true (invariants) should be enforced by the entity itself. External code should not be able to put an entity into an invalid state.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/entity-no-persistence-awareness.md`: Entities should have no awareness of databases, ORMs, or storage mechanisms. Persistence is an infrastructure detail that must not leak into the domain.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/entity-pure-business-rules.md`: Entities encapsulate enterprise-wide business rules that would exist regardless of automation. They must not contain application-specific logic, persistence code, or UI concerns.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/entity-rich-not-anemic.md`: Entities should contain behavior, not just data. Anemic domain models push business logic into services, scattering rules and duplicating validation.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/entity-value-objects.md`: Replace primitive types with value objects that encapsulate validation and behavior. Value objects are immutable, compared by value, and self-validating.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/frame-di-container-edge.md`: DI containers are infrastructure. Use them at composition root (main/startup) to wire dependencies, but don't let them invade domain or application code.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/frame-domain-purity.md`: The domain layer (entities and domain services) should have zero dependencies on frameworks, ORMs, or external libraries. Only language primitives and domain-specific code.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/frame-logging-abstraction.md`: Logging frameworks are infrastructure details. Define logging interfaces in the application layer; implement them in infrastructure. This enables switching loggers and keeps domain pure.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/frame-orm-in-infrastructure.md`: ORM-specific code (entities, mappings, queries) belongs in the infrastructure layer. The domain and application layers should not know which ORM (or if any ORM) is being used.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/frame-web-in-infrastructure.md`: HTTP-specific code (requests, responses, headers, cookies, sessions) belongs in the interface adapters layer. Use cases should be callable from any delivery mechanism.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/test-boundary-verification.md`: Use automated tests to enforce that dependency rules are followed. Architecture tests catch violations before they become entrenched patterns.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/test-layer-isolation.md`: Each architectural layer should be testable independently. Domain tests need no infrastructure. Use case tests need no web framework. This enables fast, focused testing.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/test-testable-design.md`: Clean architecture makes testing easy by design. If something is hard to test, it's a sign of architectural coupling. Don't compromise architecture for testability; fix the coupling.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/test-tests-are-architecture.md`: Tests participate in the architecture like any other component. They follow the dependency rule, couple to stable APIs, and should be designed for maintainability.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/usecase-explicit-dependencies.md`: Use cases should receive all dependencies through their constructor. Hidden dependencies (service locators, singletons, static calls) make testing difficult and hide coupling.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/usecase-input-output-ports.md`: Use cases should define their own input (request) and output (response) data structures. These ports isolate the use case from the delivery mechanism (HTTP, CLI, queue).
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/usecase-no-presentation-logic.md`: Use cases return domain data, not formatted strings, HTML, or UI-specific structures. Presentation logic belongs in the interface adapters layer.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/usecase-orchestrates-not-implements.md`: Use cases coordinate the flow of data to and from entities. Business rules belong in entities; use cases should not duplicate or implement them.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/usecase-single-responsibility.md`: Each use case class should orchestrate exactly one application-specific workflow. Combining multiple use cases creates coupling that forces unrelated changes together.
- `.claude/skills/clean-architecture/references/usecase-transaction-boundary.md`: The use case is the natural transaction boundary. All operations within a use case should succeed or fail atomically. Infrastructure manages the transaction; use case defines the scope.

