## Codemod Best Practices

Codemod (JSSG, ast-grep, workflows) best practices for writing efficient, safe, and maintainable code transformations. This skill should be used when writing, reviewing, or debugging codemods, AST transformations, or automated refactoring tools. Triggers on tasks involving codemod, ast-grep, JSSG...

- `.claude/skills/codemod/SKILL.md`
- `.claude/skills/codemod/AGENTS.md`: **Version 0.1.0** Codemod Community January 2026
- `.claude/skills/codemod/assets/templates/_template.md`: Brief explanation (1-3 sentences) of WHY this matters. Focus on performance, correctness, or maintainability implications.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/README.md`: Best practices for writing efficient, safe, and maintainable code transformations with Codemod (JSSG, ast-grep, workflows).
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/ast-check-null-before-access.md`: AST navigation methods return `null` when nodes don't exist. Always check for null before accessing properties to prevent runtime crashes.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/ast-explore-before-writing.md`: Always visualize the AST structure using AST Explorer before writing patterns. The tree structure often differs from what you expect based on source code appearance.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/ast-field-access-for-structure.md`: Tree-sitter nodes have named fields that provide semantic access to children. Use `field()` and field constraints instead of index-based child access.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/ast-understand-named-vs-anonymous.md`: Tree-sitter distinguishes between named nodes (semantic) and anonymous nodes (punctuation, keywords). ast-grep patterns skip anonymous nodes by default, which affects pattern matching behavior.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/ast-use-kind-for-precision.md`: Combine pattern matching with `kind` constraints to eliminate false positives. Patterns alone can match unintended code structures with similar text.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/edit-add-imports-correctly.md`: When adding new imports, insert them at the correct position relative to existing imports. Respect import ordering conventions.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/edit-batch-before-commit.md`: Collect all edits into an array and call `commitEdits()` once at the end. Multiple commits can cause conflicts and performance degradation.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/edit-handle-overlapping-ranges.md`: When multiple edits target overlapping source ranges, later edits may corrupt earlier ones. Detect and resolve conflicts before committing.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/edit-preserve-formatting.md`: When replacing nodes, preserve the surrounding whitespace and formatting to maintain code style consistency.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/edit-use-flatmap-for-conditional.md`: Use `flatMap` instead of `map` + `filter` when some nodes don't produce edits. Return empty arrays for skipped nodes.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/parse-early-return-non-applicable.md`: Check file applicability before performing expensive traversals. Return early when files cannot possibly contain relevant patterns.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/parse-handle-embedded-languages.md`: Code often contains embedded languages (CSS in styled-components, SQL in template literals, GraphQL queries). Use `parseAsync` to create sub-parsers for these contexts.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/parse-provide-pattern-context.md`: Some code snippets are syntactically ambiguous without context. Use the pattern object form to provide surrounding context that disambiguates the pattern.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/parse-select-correct-parser.md`: Parser selection determines AST structure. Using the wrong parser produces an invalid or incomplete AST that causes all downstream pattern matching to fail silently.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/pattern-avoid-overly-generic.md`: Generic patterns match too many nodes, causing performance degradation and false positives. Add constraints to narrow the search space.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/pattern-combine-with-rules.md`: Use rule operators (`any`, `all`, `not`) to compose complex matching logic in a single pass instead of multiple separate queries.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/pattern-ensure-idempotency.md`: Patterns should match only pre-transformation code, never post-transformation code. Running a codemod twice should produce the same result as running it once.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/pattern-use-constraints.md`: Define named constraints for commonly used matching conditions. Reference them with `matches` to keep patterns DRY and maintainable.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/pattern-use-meta-variables.md`: Meta variables (`$NAME`, `$$$ARGS`) capture arbitrary AST nodes, enabling flexible patterns that match code variations. Use single `$` for one node, triple `$$$` for multiple.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/pattern-use-relational-patterns.md`: Relational patterns (`inside`, `has`, `precedes`, `follows`) match nodes based on their structural context. Use them to avoid manual post-filtering.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/pkg-organize-by-convention.md`: Follow the standard codemod package structure. Consistent organization enables tooling support and helps contributors navigate.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/pkg-use-semantic-versioning.md`: Follow semantic versioning (semver) for codemod packages. Version numbers communicate compatibility and change scope to consumers.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/pkg-write-descriptive-metadata.md`: Write clear descriptions and keywords in codemod.yaml. Good metadata helps users find your codemod in registry search.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/security-minimize-capabilities.md`: JSSG uses deny-by-default security. Only request capabilities your codemod actually needs. Each capability expands the attack surface.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/security-review-before-running-third-party.md`: Inspect third-party codemod source code before running. Codemods with capabilities can execute arbitrary operations on your system.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/security-validate-external-inputs.md`: When codemods accept external input (parameters, config files), validate before use. Untrusted input can cause injection attacks.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/semantic-cache-cross-file-results.md`: Semantic analysis operations are expensive. Cache results when analyzing multiple related symbols to avoid redundant cross-file resolution.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/semantic-check-null-results.md`: Semantic analysis methods return `null` when symbols cannot be resolved. Always handle null results gracefully - not all code can be statically analyzed.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/semantic-use-file-scope-first.md`: Start with file-scope semantic analysis, which is fast and requires no configuration. Only upgrade to workspace scope when cross-file resolution is necessary.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/semantic-verify-file-ownership.md`: When using semantic analysis for cross-file transformations, verify that target files are within your project before editing. Never modify node_modules or external dependencies.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/state-log-progress-for-observability.md`: Add progress logging for transforms that process many files. Logs help monitor progress and debug issues.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/state-make-transforms-idempotent.md`: Transforms should produce the same result when run multiple times. This allows safe reruns after partial failures.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/state-use-for-resumability.md`: Persist migration progress in workflow state. When migrations fail mid-way, you can resume from the last successful point.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/test-cover-edge-cases.md`: Create fixtures for edge cases that production code might contain. Real codebases have unusual patterns that simple examples miss.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/test-run-on-subset-first.md`: Run transforms on a small subset of files first. Validate results manually before applying to the entire codebase.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/test-update-fixtures-intentionally.md`: Use the `-u` flag to update expected files, but always review changes before committing. Auto-updated fixtures can hide regressions.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/test-use-fixture-pairs.md`: Organize tests as paired input/expected files. The test runner compares actual output against expected files for automated validation.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/test-use-strictness-levels.md`: Use the `--strictness` flag to control how output is compared to expected. Stricter levels catch more issues but may fail on formatting differences.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/traverse-cache-repeated-lookups.md`: When transforming multiple nodes that share context, cache common lookups to avoid repeated traversals.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/traverse-single-pass-collection.md`: When you need to find multiple different patterns, combine them into a single query with `any` instead of making separate traversals.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/traverse-use-find-vs-findall.md`: Use `find()` when you only need the first match - it stops traversal immediately. Use `findAll()` only when you need all occurrences.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/traverse-use-siblings-efficiently.md`: Use `next()`, `prev()`, `nextAll()`, and `prevAll()` for sibling navigation instead of re-traversing from parent. Sibling methods are O(1) operations.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/traverse-use-stopby-for-depth.md`: Relational patterns (`inside`, `has`) traverse unbounded by default. Use `stopBy` to limit search depth and improve performance.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/workflow-order-nodes-by-dependency.md`: Define explicit `depends_on` relationships between workflow nodes. The engine executes nodes in topological order based on dependencies.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/workflow-use-conditional-steps.md`: Use `if` expressions to conditionally execute steps based on state, parameters, or previous results.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/workflow-use-manual-gates.md`: Add manual approval gates before destructive or irreversible operations. Gates pause execution until human approval.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/workflow-use-matrix-for-parallelism.md`: Use matrix strategies to parallelize transforms across teams, directories, or configurations. Independent work items run concurrently.
- `.claude/skills/codemod/references/workflow-validate-before-run.md`: Always run `workflow validate` before executing workflows. Validation catches schema errors, missing dependencies, and cyclic references.

