- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/global-conflict-resolution.md`: Configure appropriate conflict resolution policies for multi-region write scenarios. Without proper handling, data can be lost.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/global-consistency.md`: Select the consistency level that matches your application's requirements. Each level has different tradeoffs for latency, availability, and consistency.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/global-failover.md`: Enable automatic failover for high availability. Without it, regional outages require manual intervention.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/global-multi-region.md`: Enable multi-region writes for globally distributed applications. Allows writes to any region with automatic conflict resolution.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/global-read-regions.md`: Add read regions in geographic locations close to your users. Reads can be served from any region, reducing latency for global users.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/global-zone-redundancy.md`: Enable zone redundancy to protect against availability zone failures. Zone-redundant accounts distribute replicas across multiple availability zones within a region.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/index-composite-direction.md`: Every composite index entry must specify sort directions that **exactly match** the `ORDER BY` clause of the queries it serves. If the directions don't match, Cosmos DB will reject the query or fall back to an expensive scan.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/index-composite.md`: Create composite indexes for queries with ORDER BY on multiple properties. Without them, queries may fail or require expensive client-side sorting.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/index-exclude-unused.md`: Exclude paths from indexing that you never query. Every indexed path adds write cost with no read benefit.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/index-lazy-consistent.md`: Choose the appropriate indexing mode based on your workload. Consistent mode ensures query results are current; None disables indexing entirely.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/index-range-vs-hash.md`: Understand when to use different index types. Range indexes support equality, range, and ORDER BY; Hash indexes are deprecated.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/index-spatial.md`: Create spatial indexes for properties that store geographic data when you need to perform proximity or geometry queries.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/model-avoid-2mb-limit.md`: Azure Cosmos DB enforces a 2MB maximum item size. Design documents to stay well under this limit to avoid runtime failures.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/model-denormalize-reads.md`: In read-heavy workloads, denormalize frequently-queried data to avoid expensive lookups. Accept write overhead for faster reads.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/model-embed-related.md`: Embed related data within a single document when they're always accessed together. This eliminates the need for multiple queries (Cosmos DB has no JOINs across documents).
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/model-id-constraints.md`: Azure Cosmos DB enforces a **1,023 byte** maximum for the `id` property and restricts certain characters. Using non-alphanumeric characters causes interoperability problems across SDKs, connectors, and tools.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/model-json-serialization.md`: Cosmos DB stores documents as JSON. Every field on an entity that must be persisted needs to be serializable. Incorrect use of `@JsonIgnore`, missing constructors, or incompatible field types (like `BigDecimal` on JDK 17+) cause silent data loss or runtime failures.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/model-nesting-depth.md`: Azure Cosmos DB allows a maximum of **128 levels** of nesting for embedded objects and arrays. While 128 is generous, recursive or auto-generated structures can exceed this limit unexpectedly.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/model-numeric-precision.md`: Azure Cosmos DB stores numbers using **IEEE 754 double-precision 64-bit** format. This means integers larger than 2^53 and decimals requiring more than ~15-17 significant digits will lose precision silently.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/model-reference-large.md`: Use document references instead of embedding when embedded data would make items too large, or when embedded data changes independently.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/model-relationship-references.md`: Cosmos DB has no cross-document JOINs. When entities need to reference each other, store relationship IDs as persistent fields and use transient (`@JsonIgnore`) properties for hydrated object access. A service layer populates the transient properties before rendering.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/model-schema-versioning.md`: Include schema version in documents to handle evolution gracefully. This enables safe migrations and backward-compatible reads.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/model-type-discriminator.md`: Use a single Cosmos DB container to co-locate related parent/child or different entity types when: - similar entities are written and read together, share a natural or business partition key, require a simple transactional boundary, and do not exceed Cosmos DB partition key limits.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/monitoring-azure-monitor.md`: Enable Azure Monitor integration for comprehensive visibility into Cosmos DB performance, availability, and cost metrics.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/monitoring-diagnostic-logs.md`: Enable diagnostic logging to capture detailed operation data for troubleshooting. Essential for root cause analysis of production issues.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/monitoring-latency.md`: Track P99 (99th percentile) latency to identify performance outliers. Average latency hides tail latency issues that affect user experience.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/monitoring-ru-consumption.md`: Monitor Request Unit (RU) consumption to optimize costs and identify inefficient operations. Every operation has an RU cost.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/monitoring-throttling.md`: Set up alerts for HTTP 429 (Request Rate Too Large) errors. Throttling indicates your application is exceeding provisioned throughput.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/partition-20gb-limit.md`: Each logical partition has a 20GB storage limit. Design partition keys to ensure no single partition value accumulates more than 20GB.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/partition-avoid-hotspots.md`: Ensure writes distribute evenly across partitions. A hot partition limits throughput to that single partition's capacity.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/partition-hierarchical.md`: Use hierarchical partition keys (HPK) to overcome the 20GB logical partition limit and enable targeted multi-partition queries.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/partition-high-cardinality.md`: Select partition keys with many unique values to ensure even data distribution. Low-cardinality keys create hot partitions.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/partition-immutable-key.md`: Cosmos DB partition keys are immutable — you cannot update a document's partition key value in place. Changing it requires deleting the original document and reinserting with the new key, a non-atomic operation that risks data loss. Prefer creation-time values that never change.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/partition-key-length.md`: Azure Cosmos DB enforces a maximum partition key value length of **2,048 bytes** (or **101 bytes** if large partition keys are not enabled). Exceeding this limit causes write failures at runtime.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/partition-query-patterns.md`: Choose a partition key that supports your most frequent queries. Single-partition queries are orders of magnitude faster than cross-partition.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/partition-synthetic-keys.md`: When no single natural field serves as an ideal partition key, create a synthetic key by combining multiple fields.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/pattern-change-feed-materialized-views.md`: When your application requires frequent cross-partition queries (e.g., admin dashboards, analytics, frequent lookups by secondary non-PK attributes), you have two main options: use Change Feed to maintain materialized views in a separate container optimized for those query patterns, or use the ne...
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/pattern-efficient-ranking.md`: When implementing leaderboards or rankings, avoid scanning an entire partition to determine a single player's rank. Full partition scans for rank lookups are an anti-pattern that becomes unsustainable at scale.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/pattern-service-layer-relationships.md`: When using ID-based references between Cosmos DB documents (see `model-relationship-references`), create a service layer that populates transient relationship properties before returning entities to controllers, templates, or API responses. Never return repository results directly to the presenta...
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/query-avoid-cross-partition.md`: Always include partition key in queries when possible. Cross-partition queries fan out to all partitions, consuming RU proportional to partition count.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/query-avoid-scans.md`: Ensure queries can use indexes to filter data. Queries that can't use indexes scan entire partitions or containers.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/query-olap-detection.md`: **Impact: HIGH (prevents RU starvation, 429 throttling cascades, and query timeouts)**
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/query-order-filters.md`: Place most selective filters first in WHERE clauses. The query engine processes filters left-to-right, so selective filters early reduce data scanned.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/query-pagination.md`: Use continuation tokens to paginate through large result sets efficiently. **Never use OFFSET/LIMIT for deep pagination** — it is a common anti-pattern with severe performance implications.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/query-parameterize.md`: Always use parameterized queries instead of string concatenation. This prevents injection attacks and enables query plan caching.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/query-point-reads.md`: When both the document `id` and partition key value are known, use a point read (`ReadItemAsync` / `read_item` / `readItem`) instead of a query. A point read costs 1 RU for a 1 KB document and bypasses the query engine entirely. An equivalent `SELECT * FROM c WHERE c.id = @id` query costs ~2.5 RU...
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/query-top-literal.md`: Cosmos DB SQL supports both literal and parameterized values for `TOP`. Prefer parameterized `TOP` values for consistency with secure query practices. Ensure the parameter value is an integer.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/query-use-projections.md`: Select only the fields you need rather than returning entire documents. Reduces both RU consumption and network bandwidth.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-async-api.md`: Always use async/await patterns for Cosmos DB operations. Synchronous calls block threads and severely limit throughput under load.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-availability-strategy.md`: The threshold-based availability strategy (hedging) improves tail latency and availability by sending parallel read requests to secondary regions when the primary region is slow. This approach drastically reduces the impact of regional outages or high-latency conditions.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-circuit-breaker.md`: The partition-level circuit breaker (PPCB) enhances availability by tracking unhealthy physical partitions and routing requests away from them. This prevents cascading failures when specific partitions experience issues.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-conditional-create-etag.md`: **Impact: HIGH (prevents duplicate documents on concurrent or retried creates without a prior read)**
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-connection-mode.md`: Use Direct connection mode for production workloads. Gateway mode adds an extra network hop and is only needed for firewall-restricted environments.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-continuation-token-null-guard.md`: **Impact: HIGH (empty string token causes runtime `INVALID JSON in continuation token` error; `null` is the correct sentinel for first-page requests)**
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-diagnostics.md`: Capture and log diagnostics from Cosmos DB responses, especially for slow or failed operations. Diagnostics contain crucial information for troubleshooting.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-emulator-ssl.md`: The Azure Cosmos DB Emulator uses a self-signed SSL certificate that requires special handling. Additionally, **all SDKs should use Gateway connection mode with the emulator** - Direct mode has known issues with the emulator's SSL certificate handling.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-etag-concurrency.md`: When performing read-modify-write operations (read a document, update a field, write it back), always use ETags to prevent lost updates from concurrent writes. Without ETags, the last writer silently overwrites changes from other operations.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-excluded-regions.md`: The excluded regions feature enables fine-grained control over request routing by excluding specific regions on a per-request or client basis. This allows dynamic failover without code changes or restarts.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-java-content-response.md`: All Cosmos DB Java SDK point-read and write operations (`readItem`, `createItem`, `upsertItem`, `replaceItem`) return `CosmosItemResponse<T>`, **not** `T` directly. You must call `.getItem()` to extract the entity. Treating the response wrapper as the entity causes compilation errors or incorrect...
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-java-cosmos-config.md`: When configuring `CosmosClient`, `CosmosDatabase`, and `CosmosContainer` beans in a Spring Boot `@Configuration` class, use dependent `@Bean` methods with parameter injection instead of `@PostConstruct`. Calling a `@Bean` method from `@PostConstruct` in the same class creates a circular dependenc...
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-java-spring-boot-versions.md`: The Azure Cosmos DB Java SDK works with various Spring Boot versions, but each Spring Boot version has **strict Java version requirements** that must be met for the project to build successfully.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-local-dev-config.md`: When developing locally with the Cosmos DB Emulator, system-level environment variables pointing to Azure cloud accounts can override your local configuration, causing unexpected connections to production resources instead of the emulator.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-newtonsoft-dependency.md`: The Azure Cosmos DB .NET SDK requires an explicit reference to `Newtonsoft.Json` version 13.0.3 or higher. This dependency is not managed automatically - you must add it directly to your project.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-patch-counter-increment.md`: **Impact: HIGH (eliminates read-modify-write for counters; reduces RU cost and eliminates concurrency conflicts)**
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-preferred-regions.md`: Configure preferred regions in priority order for multi-region deployments. The SDK automatically routes to available regions during outages.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-python-async-deps.md`: When using the Azure Cosmos DB Python SDK's async client (`azure.cosmos.aio`), you **must** explicitly install `aiohttp` as a dependency. The `azure-cosmos` package does not automatically install `aiohttp` — it is an optional dependency required only for async operations.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-request-options-per-call.md`: **Impact: HIGH (causes wrong partition key to be sent, producing silent data corruption or 400/404 errors)**
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-retry-429.md`: Properly handle rate limiting (HTTP 429) responses by respecting the Retry-After header. The SDK handles this automatically, but configuration and logging are important.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-serialization-enums.md`: The Cosmos DB SDK's default serializer stores enums as **integers**, but many application frameworks (ASP.NET Core, Spring Boot) serialize enums as **strings** in API responses. This mismatch causes queries to fail silently - returning empty results when filtering by enum values.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-singleton-client.md`: Create CosmosClient once and reuse it throughout the application lifetime. Creating multiple clients exhausts connections and wastes resources.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-spring-data-annotations.md`: Spring Data Cosmos requires specific annotations on entity classes. JPA annotations (`@Entity`, `@Table`, `@Column`, `@JoinColumn`) are not recognized. Every entity must have `@Container`, a `String` ID with `@Id` and `@GeneratedValue`, and a `@PartitionKey` field.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/sdk-spring-data-repository.md`: **Incorrect (JPA repository patterns that fail with Cosmos):**
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/throughput-autoscale.md`: Use autoscale throughput for workloads with variable or unpredictable traffic patterns. It automatically scales between 10% and 100% of max RU/s.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/throughput-burst.md`: Cosmos DB provides burst capacity to handle short traffic spikes above provisioned throughput. Understand how it works to avoid unexpected throttling.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/throughput-container-vs-database.md`: Decide between container-level (dedicated) and database-level (shared) throughput based on workload isolation needs and cost optimization.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/throughput-right-size.md`: Provision throughput based on actual workload needs. Over-provisioning wastes money; under-provisioning causes throttling.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/throughput-serverless.md`: Use serverless accounts for development, testing, and low-traffic workloads. Pay only for actual RU consumption with no minimum commitment.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/tooling-emulator-setup.md`: Prefer the Azure Cosmos DB Emulator for local development, exploratory testing, and repeatable developer workflows. It avoids cloud cost during local work, keeps feedback loops fast, and reduces the risk of accidentally using shared or production resources while iterating.
- `.claude/skills/cosmosdb-best-practices/rules/tooling-vscode-extension.md`: For day-to-day inspection tasks, prefer the Azure Cosmos DB VS Code extension over ad hoc scripts or direct SDK calls. The extension is faster for browsing accounts, querying containers, inspecting items, and validating local-versus-cloud data without introducing disposable code into the repository.
