- `.claude/skills/cpp-embedded/references/coding-style.md`: Consistency enables grep, static analysis, and code review. Embedded codebases frequently mix C and C++ files — establish and document these conventions in your project's CONTRIBUTING.md.
- `.claude/skills/cpp-embedded/references/debugging.md`: Stack overflows are silent killers — the MCU doesn't throw an exception, it corrupts whatever memory sits below the stack. Symptoms: random crashes after deep call chains, corrupted global variables, HardFaults with no obvious cause, watchdog resets after specific operations.
- `.claude/skills/cpp-embedded/references/design-patterns.md`: Ranked by suitability for embedded, best first. Hardware is known at compile time, so prefer static dispatch over runtime polymorphism.
- `.claude/skills/cpp-embedded/references/memory-patterns.md`: An arena allocator hands out memory from a contiguous block by bumping a pointer. Allocation is O(1) and deterministic. The key trade-off: you can only free the entire arena at once, not individual objects. This is perfect for per-request or per-frame scratch memory.
- `.claude/skills/cpp-embedded/references/safety-hardware.md`: Cortex-M MPU capabilities vary by core: M3/M4 support up to 8 regions, M7 up to 16 regions. Regions are numbered by priority (higher number overrides lower on overlap). Plan region assignments carefully — you will run out.
- `.claude/skills/cpp-embedded/references/stm32-pitfalls.md`: Only code between `/* USER CODE BEGIN */` and `/* USER CODE END */` markers survives regeneration. Everything else is replaced.

