- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/_template.md`: Brief explanation of the rule and why it matters for documentation quality, readability, or discoverability.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/clarity-defaults.md`: Claude follows these by default. This checklist codifies them as the project standard:
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/clarity-meaningful-names.md`: Placeholder names force readers to mentally substitute real values. Use names that reflect the actual domain: `userId`, `orderTotal`, `configPath`.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/clarity-no-latin.md`: Use "for example" not "e.g.," "that is" not "i.e.," "and so on" not "etc." Latin abbreviations trip up screen readers and non-native English speakers.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/clarity-no-nominalizations.md`: Nominalizations bury the action in a noun. "Make a decision" becomes "decide." "Perform an analysis" becomes "analyze." "Give consideration to" becomes "consider."
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/clarity-one-idea-per-sentence.md`: Long compound sentences lose readers. Split at conjunctions. If a sentence has more than one "and" or "but," it needs splitting.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/clarity-serial-comma.md`: Place a comma before the final "and" or "or" in a list of three or more items. This prevents ambiguity.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/code-context-ratio.md`: Tutorials need more explanation (~40% code). API references are mostly code (~90% code). Don't drown tutorials in code or bury references in prose.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/code-descriptive-variables.md`: Variable names teach readers what the code does. Use `subscriptionId`, `paymentIntent`, `customerEmail` -- never `x`, `y`, `data`, `temp`, `result`.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/code-error-descriptions.md`: Don't just list error codes. Show the error message, explain what caused it, and tell the reader how to fix it. Developers reach for error docs when stuck -- help them get unstuck.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/code-isolated-to-full.md`: Show the key function first, then where it fits in the file. Let readers build understanding incrementally instead of dumping the entire file upfront.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/code-multiple-languages.md`: If your API supports multiple languages, show examples in at least the 2-3 most common ones. Use tabbed code blocks or clearly labeled sections. Don't force Python on a Node.js developer.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/code-named-functions.md`: Readers should skim function names and understand the flow. Extract named functions instead of inline logic so the top-level code reads like pseudocode.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/code-runnable-examples.md`: Readers learn by doing, not reading. Every concept, function, or API endpoint must include a complete, runnable example with imports and expected output in a comment. When adding comments in code examples, explain WHY (reasoning, constraints, non-obvious decisions), not WHAT the code already shows.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/format-bold-ui-code-font.md`: Use **bold** for UI elements the reader interacts with (buttons, menu items, page names, tabs). Use `code font` for filenames, commands, parameters, functions, and API elements. Mixing these up makes docs harder to scan.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/format-descriptive-links.md`: Link text must describe the destination and make sense out of context. Screen readers list links by text alone -- "click here" and "learn more" are meaningless in a list of 20 links.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/format-image-alt-text.md`: Every image needs alt text that conveys the information the image provides. Describe what the image shows, not what it is. Empty alt text or generic labels like "screenshot" leave screen reader users with no context.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/format-lowercase-filenames.md`: Lowercase filenames with hyphens avoid case-sensitivity bugs across operating systems. macOS and Windows are case-insensitive by default; Linux is case-sensitive. Mixed conventions cause broken links that only surface in deployment.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/format-parallel-lists.md`: Every item in a list must follow the same grammatical pattern. If one item starts with a verb, all items start with verbs; if one is a noun phrase, all are noun phrases -- mixed structures force the reader to re-parse each item.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/format-periods-inside-quotes.md`: Follow US English conventions: place periods and commas inside closing quotation marks. Place colons and semicolons outside. For code strings where punctuation accuracy matters, use `code font` instead of quotation marks to avoid ambiguity.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/format-semantic-html.md`: Use the correct HTML element for the content type: headings for sections, lists for groups, tables for tabular data, `<code>` for inline code. Generic `<div>` and `<span>` elements carry no meaning for screen readers or search engines.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/format-sentence-case.md`: Capitalize only the first word and proper nouns in headings. Sentence case is easier to read and maintain than title case -- there's no ambiguity about which words to capitalize.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/hygiene-delete-outdated.md`: Outdated documentation is worse than no documentation -- it actively misleads. Delete docs for removed features. Update docs when behavior changes. If historical context is needed, use a collapsed `<details>` section rather than leaving stale content inline.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/hygiene-docs-directory.md`: Documentation scattered across the repo is undiscoverable. Keep all docs in `docs/` (or the project's equivalent). READMEs in subdirectories are the exception -- they describe that specific directory.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/hygiene-experimental-label.md`: If a feature is experimental or in preview, add a callout immediately after the intro paragraph. Readers must know before they invest time building on an unstable API or feature.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/hygiene-no-temporal.md`: Documentation must be evergreen. Status reports, test results, meeting notes, and plans with dates belong in issues, PRs, or project management tools -- not in docs that readers expect to be current.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/hygiene-retcon-label.md`: Document-driven development sometimes means writing docs before code exists. Mark all planned or unimplemented content with `[PLANNED]` so readers don't mistake it for current behavior. Remove markers once the feature is implemented.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/hygiene-update-metadata.md`: Readers need to know if docs are current. Include a "Last updated" date or the product version the doc applies to. This helps readers assess whether the information is still accurate before they invest time.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/nav-breadcrumb-context.md`: Readers arrive at docs pages from search, deep links, and bookmarks -- not always from the top. Each page must help readers understand where they are in the docs hierarchy without requiring them to navigate there manually.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/nav-dont-repeat.md`: If a concept is already explained in another document, link to it. Don't re-explain it. Duplicate explanations drift apart over time -- one gets updated while the other goes stale, and readers can't tell which is authoritative.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/nav-every-doc-linked.md`: An unlinked document is an undiscoverable document. Every file in the docs directory must be reachable from at least one other file -- typically through an index, sidebar, or parent page. Orphaned pages accumulate and rot.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/nav-layer-depth.md`: Provide a summary for quick readers and link to deeper explanations for those who need them. Experienced readers skip the links; beginners click through. This keeps pages focused without leaving anyone behind.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/nav-relative-paths.md`: Use relative paths for links between documentation files. Absolute paths break when docs are hosted at different base URLs or built into subfolders.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/nav-searchable-headings.md`: Use the words readers actually type when looking for this information. Include the feature name or action in the heading so the heading also describes what the section contains. Generic headings like "Common issues" or "More info" don't match any search query and force readers to scan the body to...
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/review-defaults.md`: Apply these checks before publishing any documentation:
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/review-readability-score.md`: Use readability tools (Hemingway Editor, Flesch-Kincaid calculators) to check grade level. Grade 8-10 works for most technical audiences. Developer docs aimed at experts can go higher, but clarity always wins over complexity.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/review-verify-links.md`: Broken links destroy trust. After any doc change, verify that all outbound links still resolve. Check both internal doc links and external URLs. Automate this check in CI when possible.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/scan-defaults.md`: Apply these defaults to every documentation page:
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/scan-three-column-api.md`: API reference docs work best with three columns: navigation (left), description (center), and code examples (right). This lets developers read the explanation and see the code simultaneously without scrolling.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/structure-bluf.md`: The first paragraph should tell readers what the document covers and what they'll be able to do after reading it ("bottom line up front"). State the problem and the outcome in the opening paragraph. Save backstory for later or put it in a separate explanation doc.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/structure-conditions-first.md`: State where to be, what to have, or what to check before telling the reader what to do. If the instruction comes first and the condition second, readers may act in the wrong context and have to undo it. Applies to prerequisites, UI navigation, and conditional branches.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/structure-diataxis.md`: Follow the Diataxis framework: each document should be exactly one type -- tutorial, how-to, reference, or explanation. Mixing types confuses readers because each type serves a different need and reading mode. When a document drifts into another type, split it into separate files and link between...
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/structure-heading-overview.md`: Never follow a heading directly with a subheading, list, or code block. Add at least one sentence explaining what the section covers and why the reader should care. A single sentence is enough to orient the reader.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/structure-hierarchical-headings.md`: Don't skip heading levels -- go from H2 to H3 to H4 in sequence. Skipping levels breaks the logical outline, confuses screen readers, and makes the table of contents wrong. Use a single H1 for the page title and organize content under H2 sections.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/structure-next-steps.md`: End tutorials and how-to guides with a "Next steps" section linking to 2-4 related guides or logical next actions. Describe what each link helps the reader do, not just where it goes.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/structure-numbered-vs-bullets.md`: Use numbered lists only when order matters (procedures, ranked results, dependent sequences). Use bullet lists for everything else (features, options, requirements, platforms). Numbering a non-sequential list falsely implies priority or order.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/structure-one-idea-per-section.md`: Each section should advance one concept, feature, or step. Splitting keeps sections focused, scannable, and individually linkable. Test: if you can't summarize a section in one sentence, it covers too much.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/structure-procedures.md`: Start each step with an imperative verb (open, run, click, set, copy) and number sequential steps. Keep each step to one action. If a step requires a sub-action, use lettered sub-steps.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/structure-quick-start.md`: Every getting-started or README document should include a minimal Quick Start section (3-5 steps) early in the document, before detailed explanations. Readers arrive with high motivation and low patience -- let them see results immediately and link to the full guide for more context.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/voice-defaults.md`: Claude follows these by default. This checklist codifies them as the project standard:
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/voice-no-anthropomorphism.md`: Software doesn't think, want, know, try, remember, or decide. Anthropomorphic language is imprecise and can mislead readers about what the system actually does. Use precise technical verbs instead: returns, sends, rejects, creates, deletes, validates, stores, triggers.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/voice-no-jargon.md`: If a technical term is necessary, spell it out and define it the first time it appears. After the first definition, use the short form freely. Don't stack multiple undefined terms in a single sentence -- each unknown term compounds the reader's confusion.
- `.claude/skills/docs-writing/rules/voice-requirements-language.md`: "Should" is ambiguous -- readers can't tell whether it's a requirement or a suggestion. Use "must" for mandatory behavior and "we recommend" for suggestions, per RFC 2119. Avoid "please" in requirements -- it softens mandatory instructions and implies the reader has a choice.

