- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/assets/templates/_template.md`: {1-3 sentences explaining WHY this matters. Focus on animation feel and user experience implications.}
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/ease-context-matters.md`: Different animation contexts require different easing approaches. Using the wrong easing for a context makes animations feel off even when technically smooth.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/ease-custom-curves.md`: Built-in CSS easing curves (ease, ease-in, ease-out) are usually not strong enough. Custom cubic-bezier curves feel more energetic and polished.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/ease-in-out-onscreen.md`: For elements already visible that move from one position to another, ease-in-out creates natural motion by accelerating at the start and decelerating at the end—like a vehicle.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/ease-ios-drawer.md`: Drawer components should match the native iOS Sheet animation feel. The curve `cubic-bezier(0.32, 0.72, 0, 1)` with 500ms duration closely matches iOS behavior.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/ease-out-default.md`: The ease-out curve starts fast and slows at the end, creating an impression of quick response while maintaining smooth transitions. This mimics how objects naturally decelerate.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/ease-spring-natural.md`: Nothing in the real world moves with perfect easing curves. Spring animations create organic, lifelike movement that makes interfaces feel more connected to reality.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/interact-damping.md`: When users drag past boundaries, apply resistance (damping) instead of hard stops. The more they drag, the less the element moves—like stretching a rubber band.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/interact-friction-upward.md`: When users drag in the "wrong" direction (e.g., upward on a swipe-to-dismiss toast), allow movement with increasing friction rather than blocking completely.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/interact-interruptible.md`: Users should be able to change animation state at any time with smooth transitions. CSS transitions naturally support interruption; keyframes do not.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/interact-momentum-dismiss.md`: Allow users to dismiss elements with a fast flick, not just by dragging past a threshold. Calculate velocity and dismiss if either distance OR velocity exceeds threshold.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/interact-pointer-capture.md`: Use pointer capture during drag operations so the drag continues even when the pointer moves outside the element bounds.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/interact-scroll-drag-conflict.md`: In scrollable containers like drawers, dragging should only start when scrolled to the top. Add a timeout after reaching top to prevent accidental closure from scroll momentum.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/interact-snap-points.md`: Snap points should respond to velocity—fast flicks can skip intermediate points, while slow drags snap to the closest point.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/polish-blur-bridge.md`: When easing and timing adjustments don't resolve animation issues, add a subtle blur during the transition. Blur bridges the visual gap between states, masking imperfections.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/polish-clip-path-tabs.md`: Instead of animating a highlight bar separately from text color changes, duplicate the tab list with different styling and use clip-path to reveal the active state.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/polish-dont-remove-all.md`: Going nuclear and removing all animation hurts usability. Some animations help accessibility—like loading indicators and state change feedback. Provide gentler alternatives instead.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/polish-framer-hook.md`: Framer Motion provides the `useReducedMotion` hook for programmatic control over motion preferences. Use it to provide alternative animations.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/polish-hover-gap-fill.md`: When hovering should persist across a group of elements with gaps between them, use pseudo-elements to fill the gaps and maintain hover state.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/polish-opacity-fallback.md`: Opacity changes don't affect perceived position, size, or shape—they're safe for users with vestibular disorders. Use opacity-only transitions as your reduced motion fallback.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/polish-reduced-motion.md`: Animations can cause motion sickness or distract users with attention disorders. Respect the `prefers-reduced-motion` media query by providing alternative animations.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/polish-scroll-reveal.md`: Don't trigger scroll-based animations the instant an element enters the viewport. Wait until a meaningful portion (at least 100px) is visible.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/polish-stagger-children.md`: Stagger child animations to create orchestrated reveals. Children should animate sequentially with small delays rather than all at once.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/polish-toast-stacking.md`: Create visual depth in toast notifications by offsetting and scaling each preceding toast. This creates the polished stacking effect seen in [Sonner](https://github.com/emilkowalski/sonner).
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/props-avoid-css-variables.md`: CSS variables are inherited by all children. During drag animations, updating a CSS variable causes expensive style recalculation for every child element.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/props-clip-path-performant.md`: Clip-path creates reveal animations without layout shifts—elements occupy their full space while visually clipped. It's hardware-accelerated and requires no extra DOM elements.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/props-hardware-accelerated.md`: When the main thread is executing JavaScript, requestAnimationFrame-based animations (like Framer Motion) can become laggy. CSS animations and WAAPI run on the compositor thread, staying smooth regardless.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/props-transform-opacity.md`: Animating transform and opacity only triggers the composite rendering step—the cheapest operation. Animating layout properties (margin, padding, width, height) triggers expensive layout recalculations.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/props-will-change.md`: If you notice a 1px shift at the end of your animation, use `will-change: transform` to keep the element on its own compositor layer throughout the animation.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/strategy-feedback-immediate.md`: The interface should feel like it's listening to the user. Every action should have immediate visual feedback—loading states, success confirmations, error indicators.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/strategy-frequency-matters.md`: Animations that delight on first use become annoying on the hundredth. Consider how often users will see an animation before adding it.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/strategy-keyboard-no-animate.md`: Keyboard navigation and actions may be performed hundreds of times daily. Animations on these interactions make the interface feel slow, delayed, and disconnected.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/strategy-marketing-exception.md`: Marketing and landing pages are exceptions to speed rules. Users visit once or infrequently, so longer, more expressive animations are acceptable.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/strategy-purpose-required.md`: The goal is not to animate for animation's sake. Every animation should serve a clear purpose—guiding attention, providing feedback, or maintaining context.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/timing-300ms-max.md`: UI animations should stay under 300ms to feel responsive. Longer animations make interfaces feel slow and disconnected from user actions.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/timing-asymmetric.md`: Press and release actions have different purposes. Pressing should be slow to allow user confirmation; release should be fast for snappy feedback.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/timing-drawer-500ms.md`: Drawer components are an exception to the 300ms rule. The 500ms duration with iOS-style easing matches native mobile behavior users expect.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/timing-faster-better.md`: Faster animations don't just complete quicker—they make your entire interface feel more responsive and performant. A 180ms animation feels noticeably better than 400ms.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/timing-tooltip-delay.md`: Tooltips should have a delay before appearing to prevent accidental activation. Once a tooltip is open, subsequent tooltips should appear instantly with no animation.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/transform-3d-preserve.md`: Combine `transform-style: preserve-3d` with `rotateX()` and `rotateY()` to create 3D effects like card flips, orbiting elements, and depth animations.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/transform-never-scale-zero.md`: Elements animating from scale(0) feel unnatural—nothing in the real world appears from nothing. Start from scale(0.9) or higher combined with opacity for gentle, elegant motion.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/transform-origin-aware.md`: Dropdowns and popovers should animate from their trigger element, not from an arbitrary center point. Set transform-origin to match where the animation originates.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/transform-percentage-translate.md`: Use percentage values instead of fixed pixels for translateY. Percentages are relative to the element's own dimensions, automatically adapting to varying content sizes.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/transform-scale-097.md`: Add a subtle scale-down effect when buttons are pressed. A scale of 0.97 with ~150ms transition provides instant feedback that makes interfaces feel responsive.
- `.claude/skills/emilkowal-animations/references/transform-scale-children.md`: Unlike width/height changes, scale transforms apply proportionally to all child elements. This can be a feature (cohesive scaling) or a bug (unwanted text distortion).

