## Expo React Native Performance Best Practices

Expo React Native performance optimization guidelines. This skill should be used when writing, reviewing, or refactoring Expo React Native code to ensure optimal performance patterns. Triggers on tasks involving React Native components, lists, animations, images, or performance improvements.

- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/SKILL.md`
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/AGENTS.md`: **Version 0.1.0** Expo February 2026
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/assets/templates/_template.md`: Brief explanation of the rule and why it matters. This should be clear and concise, explaining the performance implications (1-3 sentences).
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/README.md`: A comprehensive performance optimization guide for Expo React Native applications, designed for AI agents and LLMs.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/anim-interaction-manager.md`: Heavy JavaScript execution during animations causes dropped frames. Use `InteractionManager.runAfterInteractions()` to defer expensive work until animations complete.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/anim-layout-animation.md`: For simple layout changes (showing/hiding, resizing), use `LayoutAnimation` instead of managing animation state. It automatically animates all layout changes in the next render using native animations.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/anim-transform-not-dimensions.md`: Animating `width` and `height` triggers layout recalculation on every frame. Use `transform: [{ scale }]` instead, which is GPU-accelerated and doesn't affect layout.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/anim-use-native-driver.md`: The Animated API runs on the JS thread by default, causing jank when the thread is busy. Enable `useNativeDriver: true` to run animations entirely on the UI thread, achieving smooth 60 FPS regardless of JS load.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/anim-use-reanimated.md`: React Native Reanimated runs animation logic on the UI thread using worklets, bypassing the JS bridge entirely. This enables smooth 60-120 FPS animations for any property, including layout properties the native driver doesn't support.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/asset-optimize-image-size.md`: Loading full-resolution images for thumbnail displays wastes bandwidth and memory. Request images sized for their display dimensions using CDN resize parameters.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/asset-prefetch-images.md`: Use `Image.prefetch()` to download images before they're needed. This enables instant display when the user navigates to a screen or scrolls to new content.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/asset-recycling-key.md`: FlashList recycles views, which can cause the previous image to flash before the new one loads. Use `recyclingKey` to ensure images update immediately when cells are recycled.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/asset-use-expo-image.md`: expo-image provides built-in disk and memory caching, BlurHash placeholders, and automatic downscaling. It outperforms the standard Image component in both performance and developer experience.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/asset-use-webp-format.md`: WebP provides superior compression compared to JPEG and PNG while maintaining quality. Serve WebP images to reduce bandwidth and improve load times.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/async-batch-api-calls.md`: Making individual API calls for each item creates N network requests. Batch items into a single call when your API supports it to dramatically reduce latency.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/async-cache-responses.md`: Repeated API calls for the same data waste bandwidth and slow down the app. Use a caching library to store and reuse responses.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/async-defer-await.md`: Start promises immediately but defer `await` until you actually need the value. This allows intermediate synchronous code to run while the promise is pending.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/async-parallel-fetching.md`: Sequential awaits create network waterfalls where each request waits for the previous to complete. Use `Promise.all()` for independent requests to run them concurrently.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/async-refetch-on-focus.md`: Mobile apps stay in background for extended periods. Refetch important data when the screen becomes focused to ensure users see current information.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/list-estimated-item-size.md`: FlashList uses `estimatedItemSize` to calculate how many items to render initially and during scroll. An inaccurate estimate causes blank cells or excessive memory usage. Measure your actual item height and provide it.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/list-get-item-layout.md`: When all list items have the same height, provide `getItemLayout` to skip asynchronous measurement. FlatList/FlashList can instantly calculate scroll position without rendering off-screen items.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/list-get-item-type.md`: When a list contains different component types (headers, items, ads), FlashList can only recycle views of the same type. Provide `getItemType` to help FlashList maintain separate recycling pools for each type.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/list-memoize-items.md`: Wrap list item components in `React.memo()` to prevent re-rendering when their props haven't changed. Without memoization, all visible items re-render whenever the parent component updates.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/list-stable-render-item.md`: Defining `renderItem` inline creates a new function on every render, causing FlashList/FlatList to think the renderer changed and re-render all visible items. Extract and memoize the render function.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/list-use-flashlist.md`: FlashList uses view recycling instead of creating new views for each item. This dramatically reduces memory allocation and improves scroll performance, especially on low-end Android devices where FlatList struggles.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/mem-abort-fetch.md`: Pending fetch requests continue even after unmount. When they resolve, attempting to update state on an unmounted component causes memory leaks. Use AbortController to cancel requests.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/mem-avoid-inline-objects.md`: Inline objects and arrays create new references on every render. When passed as props to memoized components, this defeats memoization and causes unnecessary re-renders.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/mem-cleanup-subscriptions.md`: Subscriptions to events, WebSockets, or external services must be cleaned up when the component unmounts. Without cleanup, the subscription continues running and accumulates memory.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/mem-clear-timers.md`: Timers (setTimeout, setInterval) continue running after unmount. If the callback updates state, it causes "Can't perform state update on unmounted component" errors and memory leaks.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/mem-limit-list-data.md`: Storing thousands of items in state consumes significant memory. Implement pagination or windowing to keep only visible data plus a buffer in memory.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/platform-android-overdraw.md`: Overdraw occurs when the same pixel is drawn multiple times per frame. On Android, excessive overdraw significantly impacts performance. Remove unnecessary backgrounds and flatten view hierarchies.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/platform-android-proguard.md`: ProGuard shrinks, obfuscates, and optimizes Android code. It removes unused Java/Kotlin code from your APK, reducing size and improving load time.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/platform-conditional-render.md`: iOS and Android have different performance characteristics. Apply platform-specific optimizations where they matter most.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/platform-ios-text-rendering.md`: iOS Text components can be expensive to render, especially with custom fonts or complex layouts. Use `allowFontScaling={false}` for fixed-size text and avoid unnecessary text nesting.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/rerender-derive-state.md`: When one value can be computed from another, derive it during render instead of storing it in separate state. Synced state causes extra renders and can get out of sync.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/rerender-functional-setstate.md`: When updating state based on the current value, use the functional form of setState. This eliminates the need to include state in callback dependencies, allowing truly stable callbacks.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/rerender-lazy-state-init.md`: When initial state requires expensive computation, pass a function to `useState` instead of calling the computation directly. React only calls the initializer once, but direct calls run on every render.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/rerender-split-context.md`: A single context with frequently-changing values causes all consumers to re-render. Split context into separate providers based on update frequency to limit re-render scope.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/rerender-use-callback-handlers.md`: Functions defined inside components get new references on every render. When passed as props to memoized children, this breaks memoization. Use `useCallback` to maintain stable function references.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/rerender-use-memo-expensive.md`: Use `useMemo` to cache the result of expensive computations. Without memoization, computations run on every render, even when inputs haven't changed, blocking the JS thread.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/startup-async-routes.md`: Expo Router supports async routes that split your bundle by route. Only the code needed for the current screen loads initially, deferring the rest until navigation. This dramatically reduces initial bundle size and startup time.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/startup-cherry-pick-imports.md`: Metro bundler doesn't tree-shake effectively. Importing from barrel files (index.js) pulls in entire libraries. Always import directly from the specific module path to include only what you need.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/startup-enable-hermes.md`: Hermes is a JavaScript engine optimized for React Native that compiles JavaScript to bytecode ahead of time. This eliminates runtime compilation overhead, resulting in faster startup times and reduced memory usage.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/startup-preload-assets.md`: Load critical images and fonts while the splash screen is visible. This prevents UI flicker from missing assets and ensures a smooth transition from splash to app.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/startup-remove-console-logs.md`: Console.log statements cause JavaScript thread bottlenecks in production builds. Each log serializes data and sends it across the bridge, blocking execution. Use a Babel plugin to automatically strip console statements from production bundles.
- `.claude/skills/expo-react-native-performance/references/startup-splash-screen-control.md`: Use `expo-splash-screen` to manually control when the splash screen hides. This allows you to preload critical assets and data before showing the app, preventing white flashes and incomplete UI states.

