- `.claude/skills/expo/references/bundle-remove-unused-dependencies.md`: Audit your package.json regularly for unused dependencies. Libraries installed for experimentation often remain, bloating the bundle even if never imported.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/bundle-split-by-architecture.md`: Generate separate APKs for each CPU architecture instead of a universal APK. Users only download the binary for their device's architecture.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/bundle-use-lightweight-alternatives.md`: Replace heavy libraries with lightweight alternatives that provide the same functionality. Many popular libraries have smaller, more focused replacements.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/data-abort-requests.md`: Requests continuing after component unmount waste bandwidth and can cause state update errors.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/data-cache-strategies.md`: Different data types need different caching strategies. Static data should cache longer than real-time data.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/data-optimistic-updates.md`: Update UI immediately on user action, then sync with server. Revert if the server request fails.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/data-pagination.md`: Loading all data at once causes memory issues and slow initial load. Use pagination to load data incrementally.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/data-parallel-fetching.md`: Sequential fetches multiply latency. When requests don't depend on each other, execute them concurrently.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/data-request-deduplication.md`: Multiple components requesting the same data should share a single network request, not make duplicate calls.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/image-lazy-load-offscreen.md`: Only load images when they're about to become visible. For lists, FlashList handles this automatically. For grids and custom layouts, implement manual lazy loading.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/image-preload-critical.md`: Preload images that appear immediately when a screen loads. This ensures they're ready to display without network delay.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/image-resize-to-display-size.md`: Loading a 4000x3000 image into a 100x100 avatar wastes memory and slows rendering. Resize images server-side or use expo-image's automatic resizing.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/image-use-expo-image.md`: expo-image provides built-in disk and memory caching, placeholder support, and uses performant native libraries (SDWebImage on iOS, Glide on Android) under the hood.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/image-use-placeholders.md`: Show a compact placeholder while images load to prevent layout shift and improve perceived performance. BlurHash encodes an image preview in ~20-30 characters.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/image-use-webp-format.md`: WebP provides superior compression compared to JPEG and PNG while maintaining quality. Both iOS and Android fully support WebP.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/launch-defer-non-critical.md`: Analytics, crash reporting, and other non-critical services should initialize after the first meaningful render. This prioritizes showing content to the user.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/launch-hermes-engine.md`: Hermes compiles JavaScript to bytecode at build time, dramatically reducing startup time and memory usage. It is enabled by default in Expo SDK 52+ but verify your configuration.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/launch-minimize-root-imports.md`: The root App component and its imports are parsed synchronously at startup. Move heavy imports to screens that need them and use dynamic imports for non-critical features.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/launch-new-architecture.md`: The New Architecture replaces the async JSON bridge with JSI (JavaScript Interface), enabling synchronous communication between JavaScript and native code. This eliminates serialization overhead and reduces startup latency.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/launch-preload-critical-assets.md`: Load fonts, icons, and critical images while the splash screen is visible. This ensures assets are ready when the first screen renders, avoiding layout shifts and missing content.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/launch-splash-screen-control.md`: Keep the native splash screen visible while loading critical assets like fonts and images. This prevents the jarring white flash users see when the app renders before assets are ready.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/list-avoid-inline-functions.md`: Inline arrow functions in renderItem create new function instances on every parent render, causing all list items to re-render even when data hasn't changed.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/list-avoid-key-prop.md`: Using the `key` prop inside FlashList item components breaks cell recycling. FlashList needs to reuse component instances, but `key` forces React to create new instances.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/list-batch-rendering.md`: Tune FlatList's batch rendering props to balance memory usage against scroll smoothness. The defaults are conservative and may show blank areas during fast scrolling.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/list-memoize-item-components.md`: Wrap list item components in `React.memo()` to prevent re-rendering unchanged items when the list data updates. This is essential for smooth scrolling performance.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/list-provide-estimated-size.md`: FlashList uses `estimatedItemSize` to pre-calculate scroll positions and optimize rendering. An accurate estimate prevents layout jumps and improves initial render performance.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/list-provide-getitemlayout.md`: When all list items have the same height, provide `getItemLayout` to skip asynchronous layout measurement. This enables instant `scrollToIndex` and prevents blank areas during fast scrolling.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/list-use-flashlist.md`: FlashList uses cell recycling like native iOS UITableView and Android RecyclerView, dramatically reducing memory usage and improving scroll performance compared to FlatList's virtualization.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/mem-abort-fetch-requests.md`: Use AbortController to cancel in-flight fetch requests when components unmount. This prevents memory leaks and "setState on unmounted component" warnings.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/mem-avoid-closure-leaks.md`: Closures in callbacks can retain references to component state and props, preventing garbage collection even after unmount. Use refs for values needed in long-lived callbacks.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/mem-cleanup-useeffect.md`: Always return a cleanup function from useEffect when creating subscriptions, timers, or event listeners. Failing to clean up causes memory leaks that compound over time.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/mem-profile-with-tools.md`: Use React Native DevTools, Flipper, and Hermes profiling to identify memory leaks and excessive allocations before they cause production crashes.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/mem-release-heavy-resources.md`: Explicitly release camera, video players, maps, and other heavy native resources when components are hidden or backgrounded. These don't automatically garbage collect.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/nav-avoid-deep-nesting.md`: Each navigation layer adds overhead. Keep navigation structure flat where possible and avoid nesting navigators more than 2-3 levels deep.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/nav-optimize-screen-options.md`: Define screen options statically when possible and avoid inline functions that cause re-renders. Dynamic headers should use minimal dependencies.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/nav-prefetch-screen-data.md`: Start fetching data for the next screen before navigation completes. This eliminates the loading spinner on the destination screen.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/nav-unmount-inactive-screens.md`: By default, tab screens stay mounted when inactive. For memory-heavy screens, enable `unmountOnBlur` to release resources when the user switches tabs.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/nav-use-native-stack.md`: Use native stack navigation (UINavigationController on iOS, Fragment on Android) instead of JavaScript-based stack navigators. Native stacks provide smoother transitions and better memory management.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/rerender-avoid-anonymous-components.md`: Defining components inline within JSX creates new component types on every render, causing React to unmount and remount them instead of updating.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/rerender-avoid-context-overuse.md`: Context triggers re-renders in ALL consuming components when any part of the value changes. Use context for low-frequency updates (theme, auth) and dedicated state libraries for high-frequency updates.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/rerender-split-component-state.md`: Extract frequently updating state into small, dedicated components. This prevents re-rendering the entire parent tree when only a small piece of UI needs to update.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/rerender-use-callback.md`: Wrap event handlers in `useCallback` when passing them to memoized children. Without this, new function references on every render break `React.memo`.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/rerender-use-memo-components.md`: Wrap components that receive stable props in `React.memo()` to prevent re-renders when the parent component updates but the props haven't changed.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/rerender-use-memo-values.md`: Use `useMemo` to cache expensive calculations that don't need to run on every render. This is especially important for filtering, sorting, or transforming large datasets.
- `.claude/skills/expo/references/rerender-use-react-compiler.md`: React Compiler automatically adds memoization to your components, eliminating the need for manual `useMemo`, `useCallback`, and `memo` in most cases.

