## Software Engineering Feature Specification and Planning Best Practices

Feature specification and planning guidelines for software engineers. This skill should be used when writing PRDs, defining requirements, managing scope, prioritizing features, or handling change requests. Triggers on tasks involving feature planning, specification writing, stakeholder alignment,...

- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/SKILL.md`
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/AGENTS.md`: **Version 1.0.0** Software Engineering January 2026
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/assets/templates/_template.md`: Brief description of the rule and why it matters. Explain the problem this rule prevents.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/README.md`: Feature specification and planning guidelines for software engineers, product managers, and technical leads. This skill provides a comprehensive framework for writing clear specifications, preventing scope creep, and managing requirements effectively.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/accept-avoid-over-specification.md`: Acceptance criteria should specify what, not how. Over-specified criteria constrain developers, stifle better solutions, and create maintenance burden when implementation details change.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/accept-definition-of-done.md`: Definition of Done (DoD) includes everything required for a feature to be truly complete—not just working code. Missing DoD items like documentation, monitoring, or accessibility become technical debt.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/accept-edge-cases.md`: Explicitly document edge cases and boundary conditions in acceptance criteria. Edge cases not in the spec are often not tested, leading to production bugs when users encounter unusual scenarios.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/accept-given-when-then.md`: Structure acceptance criteria using Given-When-Then (Gherkin) format. This format is unambiguous, directly translatable to automated tests, and forces thinking about preconditions, actions, and outcomes.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/accept-testable-criteria.md`: Every acceptance criterion must be verifiable with a true/false answer. Subjective criteria like "user-friendly" or "fast" cannot be tested objectively and lead to disputes about whether work is complete.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/change-defer-log.md`: When you say "no" to a feature or change, capture it in a deferred items log. Good ideas that don't fit the current scope shouldn't be lost. A well-maintained log prevents re-litigation and provides a roadmap for future work.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/change-formal-process.md`: All scope changes after requirements sign-off must go through a formal process. Without a process, changes sneak in through casual conversations, and scope creeps invisibly until deadlines slip.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/change-impact-assessment.md`: Never approve a change request without full impact assessment. Small-seeming changes often have hidden costs in dependencies, testing, and downstream effects. "Just add a button" can mean weeks of work.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/change-scope-freeze.md`: Establish scope freeze periods before major milestones. Continuous changes up until launch create instability, testing gaps, and team burnout. Freeze scope to allow stabilization.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/change-version-tracking.md`: Maintain version history for all specification documents. Without versioning, teams work from outdated specs, stakeholders reference different versions, and decisions lack audit trails.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/doc-accessible-format.md`: Store documentation where the team can find it. Inaccessible docs are useless docs. Optimize for discoverability, not organization aesthetics.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/doc-consistent-templates.md`: Use standardized templates for recurring document types. Consistent structure makes documents easier to write, review, and find information in. Ad-hoc formats waste time on structure decisions.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/doc-decision-records.md`: Record significant decisions with their context, options considered, and rationale. Without decision records, teams re-debate settled issues, and new members don't understand why things are done certain ways.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/doc-glossary-terms.md`: Create a glossary of project-specific terms. Without defined terminology, team members interpret words differently, leading to misaligned implementations and stakeholder confusion.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/doc-single-source.md`: Designate one canonical location for each document type. Multiple copies lead to version drift, conflicting information, and wasted time determining which is correct.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/prio-dependencies-first.md`: Map dependencies between features and prioritize blocking work first. A high-priority feature is useless if it depends on unfinished low-priority work. Dependencies determine actual execution order.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/prio-kano-model.md`: The Kano model categorizes features by how they affect customer satisfaction: Basic (expected), Performance (more is better), and Delighters (unexpected joy). This prevents over-investing in basics while missing delighters.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/prio-moscow-method.md`: Categorize requirements into Must have, Should have, Could have, and Won't have. This framework forces explicit prioritization decisions and creates clear expectations about what's essential versus optional.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/prio-rice-scoring.md`: When you have multiple competing features, use RICE (Reach × Impact × Confidence ÷ Effort) to score them objectively. This removes gut-feel bias and creates defensible prioritization decisions.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/prio-value-vs-effort.md`: Plot features on a 2×2 matrix of value (user/business benefit) versus effort (development cost). This visualization immediately reveals quick wins (high value, low effort) and time sinks (low value, high effort).
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/req-avoid-solution-language.md`: Requirements should describe what the system must do, not how to implement it. Prescribing solutions constrains developers and may result in suboptimal implementations.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/req-consistent-terminology.md`: When different documents use different terms for the same concept—"user" vs "customer" vs "account holder"—confusion spreads. Create a glossary defining key terms and use them consistently throughout all specifications.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/req-functional-nonfunctional.md`: Functional requirements describe what the system does. Non-functional requirements describe how well it does it (performance, security, accessibility). Both are essential; non-functional requirements are often overlooked until too late.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/req-specific-measurable.md`: Requirements must be specific enough that two developers would implement them the same way. Vague requirements like "fast" or "user-friendly" lead to interpretation disputes and rework.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/req-traceability.md`: Track each requirement from origin through implementation to testing. Traceability ensures no requirement is forgotten, enables impact analysis for changes, and proves compliance for audits.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/req-user-stories.md`: Express requirements as user stories that capture who needs the feature, what they need, and why. This format ensures every requirement is tied to real user value, not arbitrary technical decisions.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/scope-define-boundaries.md`: Explicitly state what the feature will and will not do. Ambiguous boundaries lead to scope creep, where stakeholders assume capabilities that were never planned, causing timeline and budget overruns.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/scope-define-mvp.md`: Separate the Minimum Viable Product (MVP) from the complete feature vision. This enables faster delivery of core value while deferring nice-to-have functionality to later iterations.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/scope-document-assumptions.md`: Assumptions are conditions believed to be true that haven't been validated. Constraints are fixed limitations. Documenting both early allows validation before coding starts, preventing costly mid-project discoveries.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/scope-stakeholder-signoff.md`: Require explicit stakeholder approval of scope before development begins. Verbal agreements lead to "I thought it would include X" disputes. Written sign-off creates accountability and shared understanding.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/scope-work-breakdown.md`: Break large features into smaller, estimable work items using a Work Breakdown Structure (WBS). Large, monolithic features hide complexity and lead to underestimation. Decomposition reveals true scope.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/stake-communication-plan.md`: Define how and when stakeholders will receive updates. Ad-hoc communication leads to some stakeholders being surprised while others are over-informed. A defined cadence sets expectations.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/stake-conflict-resolution.md`: When stakeholders disagree on requirements, resolve conflicts explicitly with documented decisions. Unresolved conflicts lead to passive scope creep as each party pushes their agenda during implementation.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/stake-early-feedback.md`: Don't wait until the end to show stakeholders the finished product. Share work-in-progress early to catch misalignment before significant investment. Late feedback is expensive feedback.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/stake-identify-stakeholders.md`: Map all stakeholders before requirements gathering begins. Overlooked stakeholders surface late with new requirements, causing rework. Include those who approve, build, use, support, and are affected by the feature.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/stake-success-metrics.md`: Define how success will be measured before development begins. Without agreed metrics, stakeholders will evaluate the feature by different criteria, leading to "success" debates after launch.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/tech-api-contracts.md`: Specify API contracts (request/response schemas, error codes, versioning) before coding begins. Vague APIs lead to frontend-backend mismatches, requiring costly iterations.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/tech-data-model.md`: Document data models, relationships, and migration strategies before implementation. Undocumented schema changes cause data integrity issues, failed migrations, and production incidents.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/tech-error-handling.md`: Specify how the feature handles failures before implementation. Undefined error handling leads to poor user experience, silent failures, and difficult debugging. Every external call can fail.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/tech-performance-requirements.md`: Define performance targets before implementation, not after users complain. Retrofitting performance is 5-10× more expensive than building it in. What gets measured gets optimized.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/tech-security-considerations.md`: Identify security requirements and threats during planning, not during security review. Late-stage security findings cause significant rework. Every feature that handles data needs security analysis.
- `.claude/skills/feature-spec/references/tech-system-context.md`: Before detailing feature implementation, document the system context. Show where the feature fits in the larger architecture, what it depends on, and what depends on it. Missing context leads to integration failures.

