- `.claude/skills/rust-skills/rules/anti-collect-intermediate.md`: Each `.collect()` allocates a new collection. Collecting intermediate results in a chain creates unnecessary allocations and prevents iterator fusion. Keep the chain lazy; collect only at the end.
- `.claude/skills/rust-skills/rules/anti-empty-catch.md`: Empty error handling (`if let Err(_) = ...`, `let _ = result`, `.ok()`) silently discards errors. Failures go unnoticed, bugs hide, and debugging becomes impossible. Every error deserves acknowledgment—even if just logging.
- `.claude/skills/rust-skills/rules/anti-expect-lazy.md`: Reserve `.expect()` for programming errors where panic is appropriate.
- `.claude/skills/rust-skills/rules/anti-format-hot-path.md`
- `.claude/skills/rust-skills/rules/anti-index-over-iter.md`: Manual indexing (`for i in 0..len`) requires bounds checks on every access, prevents SIMD optimization, and introduces off-by-one error risks. Iterators eliminate these issues and are more idiomatic Rust.
- `.claude/skills/rust-skills/rules/anti-lock-across-await.md`: Holding a `Mutex` or `RwLock` guard across an `.await` causes the lock to be held while the task is suspended. Other tasks waiting for the lock block indefinitely. With `std::sync::Mutex`, this is even worse—it can deadlock the entire runtime.
- `.claude/skills/rust-skills/rules/anti-over-abstraction.md`: Generics and traits are powerful but come at a cost: compile times, binary size, and cognitive load. Over-abstraction—making everything generic "for flexibility"—often adds complexity without benefit. Start concrete; generalize when you have real use cases.
- `.claude/skills/rust-skills/rules/anti-panic-expected.md`: Panics crash the program. They're for unrecoverable situations—bugs, corrupted state, invariant violations. Using panic for expected conditions (network failures, file not found, invalid input) makes programs fragile and forces callers to catch panics or die.
- `.claude/skills/rust-skills/rules/anti-premature-optimize.md`: Premature optimization wastes time, complicates code, and often targets the wrong bottlenecks. Most code isn't performance-critical; the hot 10% matters. Profile first, then optimize the actual bottlenecks with data-driven decisions.
- `.claude/skills/rust-skills/rules/anti-string-for-str.md`: Rarely. Maybe if you need `String`-specific methods:
- `.claude/skills/rust-skills/rules/anti-stringly-typed.md`: Strings are the most primitive way to represent data—they accept any value, provide no validation, and offer no IDE support. When you have a fixed set of valid values or a semantic type, use enums or newtypes. The compiler catches mistakes at compile time instead of runtime.
