- `.claude/skills/ts-google/assets/templates/_template.md`: {1-3 sentences explaining WHY this matters. Focus on the consequences of not following this rule.}
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/class-constructor-parens.md`: Always use parentheses when calling constructors, even when there are no arguments. This improves consistency and prevents potential parsing issues.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/class-no-private-fields.md`: Use TypeScript's `private` modifier instead of JavaScript private fields (`#field`). Private fields have runtime costs and interact poorly with TypeScript features.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/class-no-prototype-manipulation.md`: Never modify prototypes directly. It breaks VM optimizations, creates unpredictable behavior, and makes code difficult to understand.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/class-no-static-containers.md`: Classes with only static methods add unnecessary indirection. Export functions directly instead for better tree-shaking and simpler code.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/class-parameter-properties.md`: Use parameter properties to combine parameter declaration and property assignment into a single declaration, eliminating boilerplate.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/class-readonly-properties.md`: Properties that are never reassigned outside of the constructor should be marked `readonly` to prevent accidental mutations and communicate intent.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/control-always-use-braces.md`: Always use braces for control structures, even when the body is a single statement. This prevents bugs from misleading indentation.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/control-for-of-iteration.md`: Use `for-of` loops for arrays and iterables. Use `Object.keys()`, `Object.values()`, or `Object.entries()` for objects. Never use unfiltered `for-in`.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/control-no-assignment-in-condition.md`: Never use assignment within conditional expressions. It's difficult to distinguish from comparison and leads to bugs.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/control-switch-default.md`: All switch statements must include a `default` case, even if it's empty. All cases must terminate with `break`, `return`, or throw.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/control-triple-equals.md`: Always use `===` and `!==` instead of `==` and `!=`. The loose equality operators perform type coercion, leading to unexpected results.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/error-avoid-assertions.md`: Minimize use of type assertions (`as`) and non-null assertions (`!`). They suppress compiler checks and can hide real bugs.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/error-catch-unknown.md`: Always type catch clause variables as `unknown` and narrow before use. This prevents accessing properties that may not exist.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/error-empty-catch-comments.md`: Empty catch blocks are allowed only with comments explaining why the error is intentionally suppressed.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/error-throw-errors.md`: Always throw `Error` or `Error` subclass instances. Never throw strings, objects, or other primitives. Error instances provide stack traces.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/func-arrow-concise-bodies.md`: Use concise arrow function bodies (without braces) only when the return value is used. Use block bodies when the return value is ignored or when multiple statements are needed.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/func-avoid-this-rebinding.md`: Never use `function()` expressions that access `this`. Never rebind `this` unnecessarily. Use arrow functions or explicit parameters instead.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/func-declarations-over-expressions.md`: Use function declarations for named functions. They are hoisted, making code organization more flexible, and provide better stack traces.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/func-default-parameters.md`: Default parameter initializers should be simple values. Avoid side effects, complex expressions, or mutable default values.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/func-generator-syntax.md`: Attach the `*` to the `function` keyword with no space. For `yield*`, attach to the `yield` keyword. This provides visual consistency.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/func-rest-parameters.md`: Use rest parameters (`...args`) instead of the `arguments` object. Rest parameters are typed, work with arrow functions, and are more intuitive.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/literal-array-constructor.md`: Never use the `Array()` constructor. Its behavior is confusing (single number creates sparse array). Use array literals or `Array.from()`.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/literal-explicit-coercion.md`: Use explicit coercion functions (`String()`, `Number()`, `Boolean()`) instead of implicit coercion or unary operators.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/literal-number-formats.md`: Use lowercase prefixes for non-decimal numbers. Never use leading zeros for decimal numbers. Use underscores for readability in long numbers.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/literal-single-quotes.md`: Use single quotes for ordinary string literals. Use template literals for strings that contain interpolation or span multiple lines.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/module-es6-modules.md`: ES6 modules enable static analysis, tree-shaking, and consistent behavior across environments. Never use legacy module systems.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/module-export-api-surface.md`: Export only what consumers need. Internal implementation details should remain private to allow refactoring without breaking changes.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/module-import-paths.md`: Use relative paths (`./foo`) for imports within your project to maintain flexibility when moving code between directories.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/module-import-type.md`: When importing types that are only used for type annotations (not at runtime), use `import type` to ensure they're removed during compilation.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/module-named-exports.md`: Named exports error when import statements try to import something that hasn't been declared, catching typos and refactoring mistakes at compile time.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/module-no-mutable-exports.md`: Mutable exports create hidden state that can be modified from anywhere, making code behavior unpredictable and bugs difficult to trace.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/module-no-namespaces.md`: TypeScript namespaces create runtime objects that prevent tree-shaking and add unnecessary overhead. Use ES6 modules for code organization.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/naming-constants.md`: Use `CONSTANT_CASE` only for deeply immutable values at module scope or as static readonly class properties. Local constants use `lowerCamelCase`.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/naming-descriptive-names.md`: Use descriptive names that clearly communicate purpose. Avoid ambiguous abbreviations. Short names are acceptable only in very limited scopes.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/naming-identifier-styles.md`: Follow consistent naming conventions based on identifier type. This improves readability and makes code intent clear.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/naming-no-decorative-underscores.md`: Never use leading or trailing underscores for identifiers. Use TypeScript's `private` modifier for private members instead.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/naming-no-interface-prefix.md`: Never prefix interface names with `I` or suffix with `Interface`. TypeScript's structural typing makes these markers unnecessary.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/types-array-syntax.md`: Use `T[]` for simple types and `Array<T>` for complex types (unions, objects). This improves readability and prevents parsing ambiguity.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/types-explicit-structural.md`: Always explicitly declare structural types for objects. This catches field mismatches at the declaration site rather than at usage.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/types-no-any.md`: The `any` type allows assignment into any other type and dereferencing any property, completely disabling type checking and enabling undetected errors.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/types-no-empty-object.md`: Never use `{}` as a type. It matches almost everything except `null` and `undefined`, which is almost never the intended behavior.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/types-no-wrapper-types.md`: Never use wrapper types (`String`, `Boolean`, `Number`, `Symbol`, `BigInt`). Use lowercase primitive types. Never instantiate wrappers with `new`.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/types-nullable-patterns.md`: Type aliases must NOT include `|null` or `|undefined`. Add nullability only at usage sites. Prefer optional properties over `|undefined`.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/types-prefer-interfaces.md`: Interfaces provide better error messages (at declaration vs usage), better IDE support, and clearer semantics for object shapes.
- `.claude/skills/ts-google/references/types-prefer-map-set.md`: Use `Map` and `Set` instead of objects with index signatures for better type safety, predictable iteration order, and O(1) operations.

