## tscircuit

Build, modify, and debug tscircuit (React/TypeScript) PCB designs. Use when working with tsci CLI (init/dev/search/add/import/build/export/snapshot/push), choosing footprints, placing parts, wiring nets/traces, or preparing fabrication outputs (Gerbers/BOM/PnP).

- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/SKILL.md`
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/CHECKLIST.md`: Connectivity - All intended nets are connected; no floating power pins. - No accidental shorts between rails.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/CLI.md`: Prereqs - Node.js or Bun - Install the tscircuit CLI (global install): - npm: `npm install -g tscircuit` - bun: `bun install --global tscircuit`
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/analogsimulation.md`: Configure and run SPICE simulations for a tscircuit board.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/battery.md`: A `<battery />` is a power source that provides electrical energy through electrochemical reactions. Batteries are essential components that supply power to electronic circuits and devices. They have a positive and negative terminal and must be connected with correct polarity.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/board.md`: Root element that contains all chips and traces to create a printed circuit board.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/cadassembly.md`: A CAD assembly is a collection of cad models and constraints to "put together" a component.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/cadmodel.md`: A CAD model is a 3D model of a component that can be used in a CAD assembly.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/capacitor.md`: A `<capacitor />` stores electrical energy in an electric field. Capacitors are commonly used for filtering, energy storage, and timing circuits. Unlike resistors, capacitors can be polarized (like electrolytic capacitors) or non-polarized (like ceramic capacitors).
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/chip.md`: Used to represent virtually any single-part electronic component. Extremely flexible and supports custom footprints and pin arrangements.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/connector.md`: A general-purpose connector component for board-to-board, cable, and edge interfaces.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/constraint.md`: The `<constraint />` element is used to enforce geometric relationships between different elements in a PCB footprint. Constraints can set specific distances and alignments, such as center-to-center, edge-to-edge, or ensuring two elements line up along the same axis.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/copperpour.md`: The `<copperpour />` element (also known as a groundplane) fills large areas of a layer with copper tied to a specific net, helping with return paths, shielding, and thermal performance.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/coppertext.md`: Copper-layer text for labels, logos, and exposed copper markings.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/courtyardcircle.md`: Draw circular IPC courtyard markings around footprint features.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/courtyardoutline.md`: Draw custom polygon courtyard boundaries for irregular package geometry.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/courtyardpill.md`: Pill-shaped courtyard geometry for a custom footprint.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/courtyardrect.md`: Draw rectangular IPC courtyard markings around a footprint body.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/crystal.md`: A crystal oscillator provides a stable clock signal essential for timing applications and microcontroller operations.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/currentsource.md`: Current source component for simulation-oriented circuits.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/cutout.md`: Remove material from a board outline to create slots, notches, or other interior shapes.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/diode.md`: Diodes are semiconductor devices that allow current to flow primarily in one direction, making them ideal for rectification, signal clipping, and protection against reverse voltage. They are essential in power supply circuits and for protecting sensitive components from voltage spikes.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/fabricationnotedimension.md`: Dimension callout drawn on the fabrication layer.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/fabricationnotepath.md`: Polyline/path annotation on the fabrication layer.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/fabricationnoterect.md`: Highlight fabrication callouts and assembly regions with rectangular annotations on the fabrication layer.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/fabricationnotetext.md`: The `<fabricationnotetext />` element adds fabrication layer callouts and build notes for assemblers.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/fiducial.md`: A reference marker used for optical alignment during automated PCB assembly.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/footprint.md`: Used to define the physical layout and connection points for components on a printed circuit board.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/fuse.md`: A `<fuse />` is a safety device that protects electrical circuits by interrupting current flow when it exceeds a predetermined threshold. Fuses are essential for preventing damage to components and circuits from overcurrent conditions.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/group.md`: A group is the basic container element that can contain other elements.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/hole.md`: Used for mounting and does not have conductive properties.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/inductor.md`: An `<inductor />` stores electrical energy in a magnetic field when current flows through it. Inductors are commonly used in filters, oscillators, power supplies, and RF circuits. They oppose changes in current flow and can smooth out rapid current variations.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/jumper.md`: A simple connector that typically uses a pinrow footprint but can be used for custom layouts as well.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/led.md`: Light emitting diodes are diodes that emit light when current passes through them. They are commonly used as indicators on a circuit board such as a "power on indicator" or "data transfer in progress indicator
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/mosfet.md`: A MOSFET or "metal-oxide-semiconductor field-effect transistor" is a type of transistor that is used to control the flow of current through a circuit.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/mountedboard.md`: Place one board as a mounted child assembly of another.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/net.md`: The `<net />` element represents a bunch of traces that are all connected. You should use nets for representing power buses such as "V5", "V3_3" and "GND"
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/netalias.md`: Deprecated schematic net label alias. Prefer `<netlabel />`.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/netlabel.md`: The `<netlabel />` element attaches a text label to a net on the schematic. It replaces the old `<netalias />` element.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/opamp.md`: Operational amplifier component with standard five-pin aliases.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/panel.md`: Manufacturing panel that arranges one or more boards.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/pcbkeepout.md`: Keepout region that blocks copper/features in a PCB area.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/pcbnotedimension.md`: The `<pcbnotedimension />` element adds dimension annotations to PCBs showing measurements between two points.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/pcbnoteline.md`: Draw straight lines on your PCB for annotations, guides, and visual indicators.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/pcbnotepath.md`: Draw complex paths and polylines on your PCB using multiple connected points.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/pcbnoterect.md`: Draw rectangles on your PCB to highlight areas, create visual boundaries, and organize board sections.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/pcbnotetext.md`: Add text annotations and labels to your PCB layout.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/pcbtrace.md`: Explicit low-level PCB trace geometry.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/pinheader.md`: The `<pinheader />` element is used to create a male or female pin header with configurable spacing and number of pins.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/pinout.md`: Chip-style element used to generate or document pinouts.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/platedhole.md`: The `<platedhole />` element is used to represent a plated through hole on a PCB.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/port.md`: Define connection points within custom schematic symbols.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/potentiometer.md`: A potentiometer is a three-terminal resistor with a sliding or rotating contact that forms an adjustable voltage divider. You can also attach two terminals to create a variable resistor.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/pushbutton.md`: Pushbuttons a common type of switch normally open momentary switch. They are commonly used as a reset or pairing button.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/resistor.md`: A `<resistor />` is an extremely common element of electronic designs. It limits the flow of electricity and is critical to making sure digital signals are properly "pulled up" or "pulled down" to set their default value as `1` or `0`
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/resonator.md`: Provides a stable frequency reference for circuits, often used in clock or timing applications.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/schematicarc.md`: Draw circular arcs within custom schematic symbols.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/schematicbox.md`: Schematic-space box for grouping or callouts.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/schematiccell.md`: A single cell inside `<schematictable />`.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/schematiccircle.md`: Draw circles within custom schematic symbols.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/schematicline.md`: Draw straight lines within custom schematic symbols.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/schematicpath.md`: Draw connected line segments within custom schematic symbols.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/schematicrect.md`: Draw rectangles and boxes within custom schematic symbols.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/schematicrow.md`: A row inside `<schematictable />`.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/schematictable.md`: Draw tables within schematics.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/schematictext.md`: The `<schematictext />` element places text directly on the schematic.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/silkscreencircle.md`: Silkscreen circles are often used to indicate "pin1" on a chip.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/silkscreenline.md`: The `<silkscreenline />` element creates a line on the silkscreen layer within a footprint.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/silkscreenpath.md`: The `<silkscreenpath />` element is used to define a custom drawn path on the silkscreen layer.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/silkscreenrect.md`: Silkscreen rectangles can be used to encapsulate a rectangular area around a chip.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/silkscreentext.md`: The `<silkscreentext />` element is used to add text to the silkscreen layer within a PCB footprint.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/smtpad.md`: The `<smtpad />` element is used to represent a surface mount pad.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/solderjumper.md`: Small pads that can be cut or bridged with solder for configuration options.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/subcircuit.md`: A `<subcircuit />` is a powerful organizational element in tscircuit that represents a collection of elements that are tightly coupled. Subcircuits are often used for a small functional block, such as a voltage regulator.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/subpanel.md`: Nested grouping inside a manufacturing `<panel />`.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/switch.md`: A switch is a mechanical device that can be used to connect or disconnect a circuit.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/symbol.md`: Used to define custom schematic representations for chips using primitive drawing components like rectangles, circles, lines, and arcs.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/testpoint.md`: A `<testpoint />` is a designated location on a PCB that provides easy access for testing, debugging, and measuring electrical signals. Test points are essential for troubleshooting circuits and verifying proper operation during development and manufacturing.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/trace.md`: The `<trace />` element represents an electrical connection between two or more points in your circuit. Traces can connect components, nets, or specific pins on components.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/tracehint.md`: Autorouter hint that nudges a trace through preferred points.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/transistor.md`: A transistor is a three-terminal semiconductor device that can amplify or switch electronic signals. It is a fundamental component in many electronic circuits, including amplifiers, oscillators, and digital logic gates.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/via.md`: A via is a plated hole that connects different layers of a PCB. Vias are commonly used to route traces between layers and for thermal management.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/voltageprobe.md`: Measure voltages at specific nodes during SPICE simulation.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/elements/voltagesource.md`: Add a voltage source for SPICE simulations and schematic power inputs.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/README.md`: This folder is intended to be used as a Claude Code Skill.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/SYNTAX.md`: A circuit is typically a default export that returns a `<board />` or a form-factor board component from `@tscircuit/common`.
- `.claude/skills/tscircuit/WORKFLOW.md`: **This is a critical step for chips and ICs.** Before wiring traces, ensure your components have correct `pinLabels` and `pinAttributes`.

