- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/arch-avoid-partial-abuse.md`: **Incorrect (Partial allows incomplete objects to escape):**
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/arch-branded-types.md`: Plain `string` or `number` types allow accidental swaps between semantically different identifiers. Branded types add a phantom tag that makes each ID type unique without runtime overhead.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/arch-const-assertion.md`: Without `as const`, TypeScript widens object and array literals to mutable base types. `as const` preserves exact literal types and marks everything readonly, enabling discriminated unions, tuple inference, and compile-time validation.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/arch-discriminated-unions.md`: String enums provide no structural guarantee about which properties exist for a given variant. Discriminated unions tie shape to kind, making invalid states unrepresentable and enabling exhaustive pattern matching.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/arch-interfaces-over-intersections.md`: Intersection types recursively merge properties and can silently produce `never` on conflicts. Interfaces create a single flat object type, detect property conflicts at declaration, and are cached by the compiler for faster checking.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/arch-readonly-by-default.md`: Mutable types permit accidental mutation that causes subtle bugs — especially when objects are shared across functions. Default to `Readonly<T>`, `readonly` properties, and `ReadonlyArray<T>`, then selectively opt into mutation only where needed.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/arch-satisfies-over-annotation.md`: Type annotations widen literals to their base types, losing precise inference. The `satisfies` operator validates conformance while preserving the exact literal types, enabling autocomplete and type-safe property access.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/compile-avoid-deep-recursion.md`: Recursive types that nest beyond ~50 levels cause exponential compiler work, IDE lag, and cryptic "Type instantiation is excessively deep" errors. Flatten recursive types or add explicit depth limits.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/compile-base-types-over-unions.md`: When a union type has many members, the compiler must compare each member pairwise to eliminate redundancy -- an O(n²) operation. For unions exceeding ~20 members, use a base type with shared properties instead.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/compile-explicit-return-types.md`: Without explicit return types, the compiler must re-infer return types from function bodies on every compilation and when generating declaration files. Explicit return types on public API boundaries enable faster incremental builds and make `.d.ts` output predictable.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/compile-project-references.md`: Project references split a monorepo into independently compilable units. Each project emits declarations once and downstream projects reference those declarations instead of re-type-checking source files. This enables parallel compilation and caching.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/error-discriminated-error-unions.md`: String-based error codes and generic `Error` subclasses lose type information about error-specific data. Discriminated unions encode both the error kind and its associated data, enabling precise handling.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/error-exhaustive-error-handling.md`: Model domain errors as a discriminated union and use exhaustive switch statements to handle each variant. When a new error type is added, the compiler flags every unhandled location.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/error-never-for-unreachable.md`: The `never` type represents values that never occur. Use it to mark code paths that should be logically impossible — if the code becomes reachable due to a refactor, the compiler reports an error instead of allowing silent bugs.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/error-result-type.md`: Thrown exceptions bypass the type system — callers have no compile-time indication that a function can fail. A discriminated `Result` type makes success and failure explicit in the return type, forcing callers to handle both cases.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/error-typed-catch.md`: Caught values in JavaScript can be anything — not just `Error` instances. TypeScript 4.4+ supports `unknown` in catch clauses (and `useUnknownInCatchVariables` enforces it). Always narrow the caught value before accessing properties.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/generic-avoid-distributive-surprises.md`: Conditional types distribute over union members by default when the checked type is a naked type parameter. This causes unexpected behavior when you want to check the union as a whole. Wrap both sides of `extends` in brackets to disable distribution.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/generic-constrain-dont-overconstrain.md`: Over-constraining generics couples them to specific implementations and limits reuse. Constrain to the minimal interface needed — use the properties you actually access, not the full type.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/generic-infer-over-annotate.md`: TypeScript's inference engine handles most cases — explicit generic annotations are often unnecessary and add visual noise. Rely on inference from arguments and return types, and annotate only when inference fails or produces unwanted widening.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/generic-mapped-type-utilities.md`: When you find yourself writing multiple interfaces that are variations of the same shape (nullable version, event version, partial version), extract a mapped type utility. This keeps types in sync as the base type evolves.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/generic-return-type-inference.md`: When a generic function returns a computed type, annotating the return type with a wide interface instead of letting TypeScript infer the precise type loses generic precision at call sites.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/modern-accessor-keyword.md`: The `accessor` keyword (TS 4.9+) automatically generates a private backing field with public getter/setter. Use it when you want observable or validated properties without writing boilerplate get/set methods.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/modern-const-type-parameters.md`: The `const` modifier on type parameters (TS 5.0+) makes the compiler infer literal types by default, removing the need for `as const` at every call site. Use it for functions that need to preserve exact shapes.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/modern-noinfer-utility.md`: **Incorrect (default infers from all positions, widening the type):**
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/modern-template-literal-types.md`: Template literal types (TS 4.1+) encode string patterns directly in the type system. Use them to validate format constraints — event names, CSS units, API paths — without runtime checks.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/modern-using-keyword.md`: The `using` keyword (TS 5.2+, Stage 3 Explicit Resource Management) automatically calls `[Symbol.dispose]()` when a variable goes out of scope. This eliminates try/finally boilerplate and prevents resource leaks from early returns or exceptions.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/modern-verbatim-module-syntax.md`: **Incorrect (type import looks like value import):**
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/narrow-assertion-functions.md`: Assertion functions combine runtime validation with compile-time narrowing. After the assertion call, TypeScript narrows the type for the rest of the scope — no need for separate `if` checks or `as` casts.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/narrow-custom-type-guards.md`: Type assertions (`as`) silence the compiler without runtime verification. Custom type guards with `is` predicates combine runtime checks with type narrowing, making the code both safe and ergonomic.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/narrow-eliminate-as-casts.md`: Every `as` cast is a promise from developer to compiler with zero runtime verification. Replace casts with narrowing chains: `typeof`, `instanceof`, `in`, discriminant checks, and custom guards narrow types safely through control flow.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/narrow-exhaustive-switch.md`: When switching on a discriminated union, assign the default case to `never`. If a new member is added to the union but the switch is not updated, the compiler reports an error immediately — no silent fallthrough.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/narrow-in-operator.md`: The `in` operator narrows union types by checking for the presence of a property. This is simpler than writing a full type guard function and works well when discriminated unions lack a shared discriminant field.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/narrow-typeof-chains.md`: Accessing properties on `unknown`, union types, or external input without narrowing causes runtime errors. Chain `typeof` checks to narrow progressively — TypeScript tracks each check and narrows the type within each branch.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/perf-avoid-delete-operator.md`: The `delete` operator triggers V8 hidden class transitions, converting the object from a fast "struct-like" representation to a slow dictionary mode. Use destructuring with rest or set the property to `undefined` instead.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/perf-map-set-over-object.md`: Plain objects used as dictionaries have string-only keys, prototype pollution risks, and slower iteration. `Map` and `Set` provide O(1) operations with any key type, guaranteed insertion order, and better memory characteristics for frequently changing collections.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/perf-object-freeze-const.md`: **Incorrect (as const alone, runtime mutable):**
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/perf-object-keys-narrowing.md`: **Incorrect (Object.keys returns string[], loses key types):**
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/perf-union-literals-over-enums.md`: Regular enums emit a runtime lookup object with forward and reverse mappings, adding bundle size. Union literal types exist only at compile time — zero runtime cost, full tree-shaking, and simpler debugging output.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/quirk-empty-object-type.md`: The `{}` type does not mean "empty object" — it means "any value that is not `null` or `undefined`." Strings, numbers, booleans, and arrays all satisfy `{}`. Use `Record<string, never>` for truly empty objects or `object` for any non-primitive.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/quirk-excess-property-checks.md`: TypeScript only checks for extra properties when assigning object literals directly. Passing through an intermediate variable bypasses this check due to structural typing. Understanding this quirk prevents both false confidence and unexpected errors.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/quirk-structural-typing-escapes.md`: TypeScript's structural type system means any object with the right properties satisfies an interface — even if it has extra properties. This is by design, but it can cause data leaks when spreading or serializing objects that carry more than expected.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/quirk-type-widening-let.md`: TypeScript widens literal types for `let` variables because they can be reassigned. A `let status = "active"` becomes `string`, not `"active"`. Use `const`, explicit annotations, or `as const` to preserve literal types when they matter.
- `.claude/skills/typescript-refactor/references/quirk-variance-annotations.md`: TypeScript 4.7+ supports `in` and `out` variance annotations on type parameters. These document whether a type parameter is covariant (produced), contravariant (consumed), or invariant (both) — and help the compiler skip expensive structural comparisons.

