## TypeScript Best Practices

This skill should be used when the user asks to "optimize TypeScript performance", "speed up tsc compilation", "configure tsconfig.json", "fix type errors", "improve async patterns", or encounters TS errors (TS2322, TS2339, "is not assignable to"). Also triggers on .ts, .tsx, .d.ts file work invo...

- `.claude/skills/typescript/SKILL.md`
- `.claude/skills/typescript/assets/templates/_template.md`: Brief explanation of the rule and why it matters (1-3 sentences). Focus on performance implications.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/advanced-branded-types.md`: TypeScript uses structural typing, so `string` types are interchangeable even when they represent different concepts. Branded types add a unique marker to prevent mixing incompatible values.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/advanced-satisfies-operator.md`: The `satisfies` operator validates that a value conforms to a type while preserving the narrower inferred type. This gives you both type safety and precise autocomplete.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/advanced-template-literal-types.md`: Template literal types allow defining string patterns at the type level. TypeScript validates that strings match the expected format at compile time.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/async-avoid-loop-await.md`: Using `await` inside a loop creates N sequential operations. Collect promises and await them together, or use `Promise.all()` with `map()` for parallel execution.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/async-avoid-unnecessary-async.md`: Remove `async` from functions that only wrap a single Promise without using `await` for control flow. However, prefer `return await` over bare `return` inside try/catch blocks — it ensures errors are caught and produces better stack traces.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/async-defer-await.md`: Start async operations immediately but defer `await` until the value is actually required. This allows independent work to proceed while promises resolve in the background.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/async-explicit-return-types.md`: Explicit return types on async functions catch mismatches at the function boundary rather than at call sites. They also improve IDE performance by avoiding full function body inference.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/async-parallel-promises.md`: Sequential `await` statements create request waterfalls—each operation waits for the previous one to complete. Use `Promise.all()` to execute independent async operations concurrently.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/mem-avoid-closure-leaks.md`: Closures retain references to their outer scope variables. Long-lived callbacks can accidentally keep large objects alive, causing memory to grow unboundedly.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/mem-avoid-global-state.md`: Global variables and module-level state persist for the application's lifetime. Unbounded caches or collections at module scope grow indefinitely, causing memory exhaustion.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/mem-cleanup-event-listeners.md`: Event listeners hold references to their callback functions and bound objects. Failing to remove them when components unmount causes memory to grow with each mount/unmount cycle.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/mem-clear-timers.md`: **Incorrect (intervals never cleared):**
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/mem-use-weakmap-for-metadata.md`: WeakMap allows garbage collection of keys when no other references exist. Use it for associating metadata with objects without preventing their cleanup.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/module-avoid-barrel-imports.md`: Barrel files (index.ts re-exports) defeat tree-shaking and force bundlers to load entire module graphs. Import directly from source files to enable proper dead-code elimination.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/module-avoid-circular-dependencies.md`: Circular dependencies cause undefined values at runtime (due to incomplete module initialization) and slow TypeScript compilation as the checker resolves cycles repeatedly.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/module-control-types-inclusion.md`: By default, TypeScript loads all `@types/*` packages from `node_modules`. This causes conflicts between incompatible type versions and wastes memory loading unused declarations.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/module-dynamic-imports.md`: Dynamic `import()` creates separate chunks that load on demand. Use them for large dependencies, route-specific code, and features that aren't needed immediately.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/module-use-type-imports.md`: Type-only imports (`import type`) are completely erased during compilation, preventing unnecessary runtime module loading. Regular imports of types can force module execution even when only the type is needed.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/runtime-avoid-object-spread-in-loops.md`: Object spread (`...`) creates a new object on each use. In loops, this causes N object allocations and copies. Mutate objects directly when creating new instances isn't required.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/runtime-cache-property-access.md`: Cache deeply nested or polymorphic property access before hot loops. **Note:** Modern V8's inline caches optimize monomorphic access efficiently — this optimization is only meaningful for 10,000+ iterations with deeply nested or polymorphic properties.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/runtime-prefer-array-methods.md`: Modern JavaScript includes most common array operations. Native methods are faster (no function call overhead) and don't add bundle weight. Use native methods when they provide equivalent functionality.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/runtime-use-for-of-for-iteration.md`: **Incorrect (index-based when index isn't needed):**
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/runtime-use-set-for-lookups.md`: Array methods like `.includes()` and `.find()` are O(n) operations. For frequent lookups, convert arrays to Set or Map for O(1) access.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/runtime-use-string-methods.md`: Modern string methods like `startsWith()`, `endsWith()`, `includes()`, and `padStart()` are clearer and often faster than regex or manual substring operations.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/safety-assertion-functions.md`: Assertion functions (`asserts` return type) tell TypeScript that if the function returns, the condition is true. This narrows types in the calling scope without explicit if-checks.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/safety-const-assertions.md`: The `as const` assertion preserves literal types and makes arrays/objects readonly. This enables precise type inference and prevents accidental mutations.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/safety-exhaustive-checks.md`: Exhaustive checks ensure all union members are handled. When a new member is added, TypeScript errors on unhandled cases rather than falling through silently at runtime.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/safety-no-unchecked-indexed-access.md`: With `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` enabled, TypeScript adds `undefined` to the type of array elements and index signature properties. This catches one of the most common sources of runtime errors — accessing elements that may not exist.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/safety-prefer-unknown-over-any.md`: The `any` type disables all type checking, allowing unsafe operations to pass silently. Use `unknown` to require explicit type narrowing before operations.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/safety-strict-null-checks.md`: With `strictNullChecks`, TypeScript distinguishes between `T`, `T | null`, and `T | undefined`. This catches null pointer exceptions at compile time instead of runtime.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/safety-use-type-guards.md`: Type guards provide runtime validation that TypeScript can use for static narrowing. They replace unsafe type assertions with checked operations.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/tscfg-enable-incremental.md`: Incremental compilation caches project graph information between builds in a `.tsbuildinfo` file. Subsequent compilations only recheck changed files and their dependents.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/tscfg-erasable-syntax-only.md`: The `erasableSyntaxOnly` flag (TypeScript 5.8+) ensures your code only uses TypeScript syntax that can be removed by erasing type annotations — no code generation required. This is mandatory for Node.js `--experimental-strip-types` which strips types but cannot transform enums, namespaces, or par...
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/tscfg-exclude-properly.md`: TypeScript walks through all included directories to discover files. Overly broad `include` patterns or missing `exclude` patterns force the compiler to scan irrelevant directories, significantly slowing startup.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/tscfg-isolate-modules.md`: The `isolatedModules` flag ensures each file can be transpiled independently, enabling parallel transpilation by bundlers like esbuild, swc, or Babel. This bypasses TypeScript's slower multi-file analysis.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/tscfg-isolated-declarations.md`: The `isolatedDeclarations` flag (TypeScript 5.5+) ensures each file's exports are annotated sufficiently for tools to generate `.d.ts` files without running the type-checker. This enables parallel declaration emit via bundlers and dramatically speeds up builds in large codebases.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/tscfg-project-references.md`: Project references split a codebase into independent compilation units. Each project compiles separately, enabling parallel builds and preventing the compiler from loading the entire codebase at once.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/tscfg-skip-lib-check.md`: The `skipLibCheck` option skips type-checking of declaration files (`.d.ts`). Since these files are pre-verified by library authors, checking them is redundant and wastes compilation time.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/tscfg-strict-function-types.md`: With `strictFunctionTypes` enabled, TypeScript uses fast variance-based checking for function parameters. Without it, TypeScript falls back to slower structural comparison for every function type.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/type-avoid-deep-generics.md`: Each layer of generic nesting multiplies type instantiation cost. Flatten generic hierarchies or use intermediate type aliases to reduce the combinatorial explosion of type checking.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/type-avoid-large-unions.md`: Union type checking is quadratic — TypeScript compares each union member pairwise. Unions with 50+ elements cause measurable compilation slowdowns and IDE lag. This commonly occurs with generated types (GraphQL schemas, API response codes, database enums).
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/type-explicit-return-types.md`: Explicit return types accelerate compilation by eliminating inference overhead. Named types are more compact than inferred anonymous types, speeding up declaration file generation and consumption.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/type-extract-conditional-types.md`: Inline conditional types are re-evaluated on every function call. Extracting them to named type aliases allows the compiler to cache results and reuse them across multiple call sites.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/type-interfaces-over-intersections.md`: Interfaces create a single flat object type that detects property conflicts at declaration. Intersections recursively merge properties on every use, forcing the compiler to recompute the combined type repeatedly.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/type-limit-recursion-depth.md`: Recursive types without depth limits can cause exponential type expansion, leading to compilation hangs or out-of-memory errors. Add explicit depth counters or use tail-recursive patterns.
- `.claude/skills/typescript/references/type-simplify-mapped-types.md`: Overly complex mapped types with multiple conditional branches slow compilation significantly. Break them into smaller, focused utility types and compose them.

