- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/assets/templates/_template.md`: Brief explanation (1-3 sentences) of WHY this matters. Focus on the performance or correctness implications for CLI tools.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/args-never-read-secrets-from-flags.md`: Command-line arguments appear in process listings (`ps aux`), shell history, and logs. Never accept passwords, API keys, or tokens as flag values.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/args-prefer-flags-over-positional.md`: Use named flags instead of positional arguments when the meaning isn't obvious. Flags are self-documenting, order-independent, and allow adding new options without breaking existing scripts.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/args-provide-long-options.md`: Every single-letter option should have an equivalent long-form option. Long options are self-documenting in scripts and easier to remember.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/args-require-help-version.md`: Every CLI must support `--help` and `--version`. These are universally expected and required by GNU Coding Standards. Exit with status 0 after displaying either.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/args-support-double-dash.md`: The `--` argument terminates option processing, allowing operands that start with `-` to be treated as filenames rather than options. This is required by POSIX and essential for safe file handling.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/args-support-option-bundling.md`: Allow multiple single-letter options to be combined behind one hyphen. This is standard POSIX behavior and users expect it to work.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/args-use-getopt.md`: Use `getopt`, `getopt_long`, or equivalent libraries instead of manual argument parsing. These libraries handle edge cases, provide consistent behavior, and generate proper error messages.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/args-use-standard-flag-names.md`: Use conventional flag names that users already know. Consistency across tools reduces cognitive load and prevents errors.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/config-env-naming.md`: Prefix environment variables with your program name and use SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE. This prevents conflicts and makes variables discoverable.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/config-follow-xdg.md`: Store configuration in XDG directories (`~/.config/appname/`) instead of dotfiles in home. This keeps home directories clean and enables per-user and system-wide configurations.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/config-never-store-secrets.md`: Config files get committed to version control. Environment variables appear in process listings and logs. Use dedicated secret storage mechanisms.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/config-precedence-order.md`: Apply configuration from multiple sources in a consistent, predictable order. Command-line flags should override everything else.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/config-respect-standard-vars.md`: Honor standard environment variables like `NO_COLOR`, `EDITOR`, `PAGER`, and proxy settings. This ensures your tool integrates well with the user's environment.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/error-actionable-messages.md`: Error messages should tell users what went wrong AND how to fix it. A good error message saves users from searching documentation or Stack Overflow.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/error-avoid-stack-traces.md`: Stack traces are useful for developers but overwhelming for users. Show clean error messages by default; reserve stack traces for debug mode.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/error-include-program-name.md`: Prefix error messages with the program name. When multiple tools run in a pipeline or script, this identifies which tool produced the error.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/error-use-strerror.md`: When system calls fail, use `strerror(errno)` or `perror()` to provide the standard system error message. These messages are consistent, localized, and familiar to users.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/error-validate-early.md`: Validate all inputs before starting work. Failing halfway through an operation can leave data in an inconsistent state and waste user time.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/exit-distinguish-error-types.md`: Use different exit codes for different error categories so scripts can respond appropriately. A config error should be distinguishable from a network error.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/exit-partial-success.md`: When processing multiple inputs, return non-zero if any operation failed. Use a consistent strategy: either fail fast or process all and report aggregate status.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/exit-signal-codes.md`: When a program terminates due to a signal, it should exit with code 128 plus the signal number. This convention allows parent processes to determine the exact cause of termination.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/exit-use-standard-codes.md`: Use the BSD sysexits.h conventions for exit codes. These provide semantic meaning that scripts can act upon. Avoid inventing custom codes that conflict with reserved values.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/exit-zero-for-success.md`: Exit code 0 means success and nothing else. Any non-zero exit code indicates failure. Scripts, CI/CD pipelines, and shell operators (`&&`, `||`) depend on this convention.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/help-include-examples.md`: Include 2-4 real-world examples in your `--help` output. Examples teach faster than abstract descriptions.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/help-show-defaults.md`: Display default values for all options that have them. Users should know what happens without specifying each option.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/help-show-usage-on-error.md`: When arguments are missing or invalid, show a brief usage hint along with the error. Don't force users to run `--help` separately.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/help-structure-help-output.md`: Follow the standard help output structure: synopsis, description, options (with defaults), examples, and where to report bugs.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/help-version-format.md`: The `--version` output should include the program name, version number, copyright, and license. Follow the GNU format for consistency.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/io-atomic-writes.md`: Write to a temporary file and rename it to the final destination. This prevents partial writes from corrupting data if the program crashes or is interrupted.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/io-be-stateless.md`: Prefer stateless operations that don't depend on hidden state. Given the same inputs, the tool should produce the same outputs. This enables reliable automation and easy recovery from failures.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/io-handle-binary-safely.md`: When processing binary data, use binary mode for file operations and avoid functions that assume text encoding. Text-mode I/O can corrupt binary data.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/io-handle-multiple-files.md`: Accept multiple input files and process them as a stream, like `cat`, `grep`, and other standard tools. Support shell globbing patterns naturally.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/io-support-stdin.md`: When no input file is specified, read from stdin. This enables your tool to work as a filter in pipelines, a core UNIX pattern.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/io-write-to-stdout.md`: Write output to stdout unless an output file is explicitly specified with `-o`/`--output`. Let users redirect output with standard shell operators.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/output-detect-tty.md`: Check if stdout is a terminal before adding colors, progress bars, or interactive formatting. When output is piped or redirected, use plain machine-readable output.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/output-line-based-text.md`: Default output should be one record per line, terminated by newlines. This enables processing with standard tools like `grep`, `awk`, `sort`, and `head`.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/output-provide-machine-format.md`: Offer a `--json`, `--porcelain`, or similar flag for structured, machine-parseable output. Human-readable output changes; machine output is a stable API.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/output-respect-no-color.md`: When `NO_COLOR` environment variable is set (to any value), disable all color output. This is a cross-tool standard for accessibility and compatibility.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/output-stderr-for-errors.md`: All error messages, warnings, progress indicators, and diagnostic information must go to stderr. This allows stdout to be cleanly piped or redirected.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/output-stdout-for-data.md`: Standard output is for program data that can be piped to other programs. Progress messages, warnings, and any human-oriented text must go to stderr. Mixing them breaks pipes.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/signal-cleanup-on-second-interrupt.md`: If cleanup is taking too long, a second Ctrl-C should exit immediately. Don't trap users in a slow cleanup process.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/signal-handle-sigint.md`: When the user presses Ctrl-C (SIGINT), exit promptly after minimal cleanup. Don't ignore the signal or take too long to exit.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/signal-handle-sigpipe.md`: Ignore SIGPIPE and handle write errors explicitly. Default SIGPIPE behavior terminates the program when the pipe reader closes early.
- `.claude/skills/unix-cli/references/signal-handle-sigterm.md`: Handle SIGTERM to allow graceful shutdown when killed by process managers, init systems, or container orchestrators.

