- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/examples/safe-abstraction.md`: Examples of building safe APIs on top of unsafe code.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/_sections.md`: **Focus**: When and why to use unsafe
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/_template.md`: Use this template for all unsafe-checker rules.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-01-no-string-direct.md`: Use `CString` and `CStr` for string handling at FFI boundaries. Never pass Rust `String` or `&str` directly to C.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-02-read-ffi-docs.md`: The `std::ffi` module has many types with subtle differences. Read their documentation carefully to avoid misuse.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-03-drop-for-c-ptr.md`: When wrapping a C pointer that owns memory, implement `Drop` to call the appropriate C deallocation function.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-04-panic-boundary.md`: Panics must not unwind across FFI boundaries. Use `catch_unwind` or mark functions as `extern "C-unwind"`.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-05-portable-types.md`: Use type aliases from `std::os::raw` or the `libc` crate for C-compatible types. Don't assume sizes of C types.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-06-string-abi.md`: When passing strings across FFI, ensure both sides agree on encoding, null-termination, and memory ownership.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-07-no-drop-external.md`: If a type will be passed to external code that manages its lifetime, don't implement `Drop`. Otherwise, both Rust and the external code will try to free it.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-08-error-handling.md`: FFI functions must use C-compatible error handling (return codes, errno, out parameters). Rust's Result/Option don't cross FFI boundaries.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-09-ref-not-ptr.md`: When wrapping C functions that don't need null pointers, use Rust references in the safe wrapper to enforce non-null at compile time.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-10-thread-safety.md`: Functions exported to C with `#[no_mangle] extern "C"` may be called from multiple threads. Ensure they are thread-safe.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-11-packed-ub.md`: Creating references to fields in `#[repr(packed)]` structs is undefined behavior if the field is misaligned. Use raw pointers and `read_unaligned`/`write_unaligned` instead.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-12-invariant-doc.md`: When receiving parameters from C, document what invariants you assume (non-null, alignment, validity, lifetime) and verify them when possible.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-13-data-layout.md`: Types shared between Rust and C must have `#[repr(C)]` to ensure the memory layout matches what C expects.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-14-stable-layout.md`: FFI types should not change layout between versions. Use `#[repr(C)]` and avoid types with unstable layout like generic `std` types.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-15-validate-external.md`: Data received from external sources (FFI, files, network) may be invalid. Validate before using it as Rust types with stricter invariants.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-16-closure-to-c.md`: C callbacks are function pointers without captured state. To pass Rust closures to C, separate the function pointer from the closure data using a "trampoline" pattern.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-17-opaque-types.md`: Instead of using `*mut c_void` for opaque C handles, create dedicated marker types that provide type safety.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ffi-18-no-trait-objects.md`: Trait objects (`dyn Trait`) have Rust-specific layout (fat pointers with vtable) that is not compatible with C.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/general-01-no-abuse.md`: Unsafe Rust should not be used as an escape hatch from the borrow checker or other compiler safety mechanisms.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/general-02-not-for-perf.md`: Do not assume that using `unsafe` will automatically improve performance. Always measure first and verify the safety invariants.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/general-03-no-alias.md`: Do not create type aliases, re-exports, or wrapper methods that hide the "unsafe" nature of operations.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/io-01-raw-handle.md`: When working with raw file descriptors or handles, ensure they are valid for the duration of use and properly ownership-tracked.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/mem-01-repr-layout.md`: Use `#[repr(...)]` attributes to control data layout when interfacing with C, doing memory mapping, or needing specific guarantees.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/mem-02-no-other-process.md`: Do not directly manipulate memory belonging to other processes or dynamically loaded libraries. Use proper IPC or FFI mechanisms.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/mem-03-no-auto-drop-foreign.md`: Never create `String`, `Vec`, or `Box` from memory allocated outside Rust's allocator. They will try to free the memory with the wrong deallocator.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/mem-04-reentrant.md`: When calling C functions or system calls, use reentrant (`_r`) versions to avoid data races from global state.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/mem-05-bitfield-crates.md`: Use crates like `bitflags`, `bitvec`, or `modular-bitfield` instead of manual bit manipulation for complex bitfield operations.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/mem-06-maybeuninit.md`: Use `MaybeUninit<T>` instead of `mem::uninitialized()` or `mem::zeroed()` when working with uninitialized memory.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ptr-01-no-thread-share.md`: Raw pointers (`*const T`, `*mut T`) are not `Send` or `Sync` by default. Do not share them across threads without ensuring proper synchronization.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ptr-02-prefer-nonnull.md`: Use `NonNull<T>` instead of `*mut T` when the pointer should never be null. This enables null pointer optimization and makes the intent clear.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ptr-03-phantomdata.md`: When a struct contains raw pointers but logically owns or borrows the pointed-to data, use `PhantomData<T>` to tell the compiler about the relationship.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ptr-04-alignment.md`: When casting a pointer to a different type, ensure the resulting pointer is properly aligned for the target type.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ptr-05-no-const-to-mut.md`: Never cast `*const T` to `*mut T` and dereference it to write. This violates aliasing rules and is undefined behavior.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/ptr-06-prefer-cast.md`: Use the `cast()` method instead of `as` for pointer type conversions. It's clearer and prevents accidental provenance loss.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/safety-01-panic-safety.md`: Panics in unsafe code can leave data structures in an inconsistent state, leading to undefined behavior when the panic is caught.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/safety-02-verify-invariants.md`: When writing unsafe code, you are taking responsibility for upholding all safety invariants that the compiler normally enforces.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/safety-03-no-uninit-api.md`: Public APIs must never return or expose uninitialized memory to callers.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/safety-04-double-free.md`: Ensure that resources are not freed twice, especially when panics can occur during operations.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/safety-05-send-sync.md`: When manually implementing `Send` or `Sync`, you must ensure thread safety invariants are upheld.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/safety-06-no-raw-ptr-api.md`: Public APIs should use safe abstractions (references, slices, smart pointers) instead of exposing raw pointers.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/safety-07-unsafe-pair.md`: When providing performance-critical operations that skip safety checks, offer both a safe checked version and an unsafe unchecked version.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/safety-08-no-mut-from-immut.md`: A function taking `&self` or `&T` must not return `&mut T` to the same data without interior mutability.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/safety-09-safety-comment.md`: Every `unsafe` block or `unsafe impl` must have a `// SAFETY:` comment explaining why the operation is safe.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/safety-10-safety-doc.md`: Public `unsafe` functions must have a `# Safety` section in their documentation explaining the caller's obligations.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/safety-11-assert-not-debug.md`: In `unsafe` functions or functions containing unsafe blocks, prefer `assert!` over `debug_assert!` for checking safety invariants.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/union-01-avoid-except-ffi.md`: Only use `union` for FFI with C code. For Rust-only code, use `enum` with explicit tags.
- `.claude/skills/unsafe-checker/rules/union-02-no-cross-lifetime.md`: Do not write to one union field and read from another field that has a different lifetime or references data with a different lifetime.

