- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/README.md`: Created by [WOW - Wielka Optymalizacja WordPressa](https://www.wielkaoptymalizacjawordpressa.pl/), Bartek Miś
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/_sections.md`: **Impact:** CRITICAL **Description:** Database queries are the primary bottleneck in WordPress performance. Unoptimized queries, missing indexes, direct database access, and improper use of WP_Query can cause severe slowdowns, especially on high-traffic sites. Following WordPress VIP standards fo...
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/_template.md`: Brief explanation of the rule and why it matters for WordPress performance. Include context about when this applies and the consequences of not following this practice.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/advanced-autoload-optimization.md`: WordPress loads all autoloaded options on every page request. Excessive autoloaded data (over 800KB) slows down every page load. Set autoload to 'no' for options that aren't needed on every request, and regularly audit autoloaded options.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/advanced-cron-optimization.md`: WP-Cron runs on page loads, which can slow down user requests. For high-traffic sites, disable WP-Cron's page-load trigger and use system cron instead. Schedule events wisely and clean up unused scheduled events.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/advanced-memory-management.md`: Large operations can exhaust PHP's memory limit. Monitor memory usage, process data in batches, and clean up resources when done. This is especially important for imports, exports, and batch operations.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/advanced-query-monitor.md`: Use profiling tools to identify actual performance bottlenecks rather than optimizing blindly. Query Monitor, Debug Bar, and New Relic help pinpoint slow queries, excessive hooks, and memory issues. Profile in production-like environments.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/api-admin-ajax.md`: Admin-ajax.php loads the entire WordPress admin environment, making it slow for frontend AJAX requests. For frontend functionality, use the REST API instead. Reserve admin-ajax for admin-only features.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/api-nonce-validation.md`: Use WordPress nonces correctly to prevent CSRF attacks. Create nonces with specific actions, validate them on the receiving end, and understand their limitations. Don't over-rely on nonces for authentication.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/api-rest-optimization.md`: Design REST API endpoints for performance: return only needed fields, implement pagination, add proper caching headers, and use efficient queries. Avoid N+1 queries and unnecessary data serialization.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/asset-conditional-loading.md`: Only load scripts and styles on pages where they're actually needed. Loading all assets on every page wastes bandwidth and slows down pages. Use WordPress conditional tags to selectively enqueue assets.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/asset-defer-async.md`: Add `defer` or `async` attributes to non-critical scripts to prevent them from blocking page rendering. Since WordPress 6.3, use the `strategy` parameter in `wp_register_script()` and `wp_enqueue_script()` for clean defer/async support.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/asset-dequeue-unused.md`: Many plugins load their assets on every page, even when not needed. Identify and dequeue unused plugin scripts and styles on pages where they're not required. This reduces HTTP requests and page weight.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/asset-minification.md`: Minify CSS and JavaScript files to reduce file sizes. With HTTP/2, combining files is less critical than before, but minification remains important. Use build tools for development and consider caching plugins for production.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/asset-proper-enqueue.md`: Always use `wp_enqueue_script()` and `wp_enqueue_style()` to load assets. Never hardcode script or link tags in templates. Proper enqueueing enables dependency management, versioning, conditional loading, and prevents duplicate asset loading.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/cache-fragment-caching.md`: Cache expensive template fragments (widgets, complex loops, sidebars) separately from full-page caching. This allows dynamic pages to still benefit from caching static portions. Fragment caching is especially useful for logged-in users where page caching isn't possible.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/cache-invalidation.md`: Cache invalidation should be precise and event-driven. Clear only the specific cached data that changed, not entire caches. Hook into WordPress actions to automatically invalidate caches when underlying data changes.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/cache-object-cache.md`: Use `wp_cache_*` functions with cache groups for better organization and selective invalidation. Cache groups allow you to clear related cached items without flushing the entire cache. This is especially important on sites with persistent object caching (Redis, Memcached).
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/cache-remote-requests.md`: Always cache responses from `wp_remote_get()`, `wp_remote_post()`, and other HTTP API functions. Remote requests add significant latency (100ms-2s+) and can fail, causing page load delays. Cache successful responses and handle failures gracefully.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/cache-transients-proper-use.md`: Transients are ideal for caching expensive external operations like API calls, remote requests, and complex computations. They persist across page loads and work with or without a persistent object cache. Never use transients for data that originates from the WordPress database.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/db-avoid-post-not-in.md`: The `post__not_in` parameter in WP_Query creates a `NOT IN` SQL clause that cannot use indexes effectively. On sites with large posts tables, this causes full table scans and severely impacts performance. This is explicitly flagged by WordPress VIP coding standards.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/db-fields-optimization.md`: When you only need post IDs or specific fields, use the `fields` parameter to avoid fetching complete post objects. This significantly reduces memory usage and query time, especially when processing large numbers of posts.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/db-limit-query-results.md`: Never use `posts_per_page => -1` or `nopaging => true` in production code. Unlimited queries can return thousands of posts, causing memory exhaustion, slow page loads, and server timeouts. Always set reasonable limits and implement pagination.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/db-meta-query-indexing.md`: Meta queries (`meta_query` in WP_Query) can be slow because the postmeta table's default indexes don't cover all query patterns. For frequently queried meta keys, consider adding custom indexes or using alternative data structures like taxonomies.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/db-prepared-statements.md`: Always use `$wpdb->prepare()` when executing database queries with user input or dynamic values. This prevents SQL injection attacks and allows the database to cache query execution plans. Never concatenate variables directly into SQL strings.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/db-use-wp-query.md`: Always use WordPress API functions (WP_Query, get_posts, get_post_meta) instead of direct `$wpdb` queries when fetching WordPress data. WordPress APIs leverage the object cache, handle data sanitization, and maintain plugin compatibility through filters.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/media-image-sizes.md`: Register custom image sizes that match your design requirements. Don't use 'full' size when a smaller size would work. Properly named sizes make development clearer and ensure optimized images are served.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/media-lazy-loading.md`: Use native lazy loading for images and iframes below the fold. WordPress 5.5+ adds `loading="lazy"` by default. Don't lazy load above-the-fold images (especially LCP images) as this hurts Core Web Vitals.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/media-responsive-images.md`: Use WordPress's built-in responsive image support with srcset and sizes attributes. This ensures browsers download appropriately sized images for each device, saving bandwidth and improving load times.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/plugin-activation-hooks.md`: Use activation hooks for one-time setup tasks like creating database tables, setting default options, and scheduling cron jobs. Don't perform these tasks on every page load. Use deactivation hooks to clean up scheduled events.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/plugin-autoloading.md`: Use PHP autoloading instead of manually requiring files. Autoloading ensures classes are only loaded when actually used, reducing memory consumption and improving performance. Use Composer's autoloader or implement PSR-4 compatible autoloading.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/plugin-conditional-loading.md`: Don't load all plugin code on every request. Use conditional checks to only load code when it's actually needed. Frontend code shouldn't load in admin, admin code shouldn't load on frontend, and feature-specific code should only load when that feature is used.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/plugin-hook-removal.md`: When you need to remove hooks added by WordPress core, themes, or other plugins, use `remove_action()` and `remove_filter()` correctly. Match the exact function reference and priority. Use proper timing to ensure hooks are removed after they're added.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/theme-avoid-queries-in-templates.md`: Keep database queries out of template files. Templates should only display data, not fetch it. Move queries to functions.php, controllers, or use pre_get_posts to modify the main query. This improves maintainability and prevents accidental performance issues.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/theme-hooks-placement.md`: Use appropriate priority levels when adding actions and filters. Lower numbers run first. Understanding hook priority prevents conflicts with plugins and ensures your code runs at the right time.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/theme-loop-optimization.md`: Optimize loops to prevent N+1 queries and memory issues. Use `update_post_meta_cache` and `update_post_term_cache` parameters, reset post data properly, and avoid expensive operations inside loops.
- `.claude/skills/wordpress-performance-best-practices/rules/theme-template-parts.md`: Use `get_template_part()` to create reusable template components. Pass data using the third parameter (WordPress 5.5+) instead of relying on global variables. This creates cleaner, more maintainable, and cacheable template code.

