- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/assets/templates/_template.md`: Brief explanation (1-3 sentences) of WHY this matters. Focus on performance implications or the problem being solved.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/README.md`: A comprehensive performance optimization guide for WXT browser extension development, containing 49 rules across 8 categories. Updated for WXT v0.20+.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/_sections.md`: This file defines all sections, their ordering, impact levels, and descriptions. The section ID (in parentheses) is the filename prefix used to group rules.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/bundle-analyze-size.md`: Use bundle analysis to identify unexpectedly large dependencies. Many npm packages include unnecessary code that dramatically increases extension size.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/bundle-externalize-wasm.md`: WebAssembly modules and large assets should be loaded on-demand, not bundled into entry files. This keeps initial load fast for users who don't need heavy features.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/bundle-minify-content-scripts.md`: Content scripts run on every matching page. Large content scripts slow down page loads across all affected sites. Keep them minimal.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/bundle-split-entrypoints.md`: Each WXT entrypoint (background, popup, content script) should only import what it needs. Shared code between entrypoints bloats all bundles.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/bundle-tree-shake-icons.md`: Icon libraries like Lucide, Heroicons, and FontAwesome contain thousands of icons. Import individual icons directly instead of from barrel files.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/inject-all-frames.md`: By default, content scripts only run in the top frame. Set `allFrames: true` when you need to process content in iframes, but be aware of the performance cost.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/inject-choose-correct-world.md`: Content scripts can run in ISOLATED (default) or MAIN world. ISOLATED has extension API access but cannot see page variables. MAIN can see page variables but loses extension API access.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/inject-dynamic-registration.md`: Use runtime registration instead of manifest registration when content scripts should only inject after user grants optional permissions or based on user settings.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/inject-run-at-timing.md`: The `runAt` option controls when content scripts execute. Wrong timing causes race conditions where scripts run before elements exist or after events have fired.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/inject-spa-navigation.md`: Content scripts don't re-execute on SPA (Single Page Application) navigation because the page doesn't fully reload. Use WXT's `wxt:locationchange` event to detect URL changes and re-run logic.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/inject-use-ctx-invalidated.md`: When an extension updates, existing content scripts become "orphaned" - they still run but can't communicate with the new background script. Use WXT's context to detect invalidation and clean up.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/inject-use-main-function.md`: WXT imports entrypoint files in a Node.js build environment where browser APIs don't exist. All code using `browser.*` APIs must be inside the `main()` function.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/manifest-content-security-policy.md`: MV3 enforces strict CSP for extension pages. Configure CSP to allow necessary functionality while maintaining security.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/manifest-cross-browser-compatibility.md`: WXT can build for multiple browsers from single codebase. Use browser-specific configuration and conditional code for compatibility.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/manifest-minimal-permissions.md`: Request only the permissions your extension actually needs. Excessive permissions trigger Chrome Web Store review delays and reduce user trust.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/manifest-use-optional-permissions.md`: Use optional permissions to reduce install-time warnings. Request additional permissions only when the user needs the associated feature.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/manifest-web-accessible-resources.md`: Web accessible resources can be accessed by any web page. Limit them to specific domains to prevent extension fingerprinting and unauthorized resource access.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/msg-avoid-circular-messages.md`: Message handlers that send messages can create infinite loops. Use message type checks and sender validation to prevent circular message patterns.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/msg-handle-no-receiver.md`: **Incorrect (unhandled rejection):**
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/msg-return-true-for-async.md`: When handling messages asynchronously with the raw `browser.runtime.onMessage` API, you must return `true` synchronously to keep the message channel open. Without this, `sendResponse` is called before your async work completes.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/msg-type-safe-messaging.md`: Use `@webext-core/messaging` for type-safe messaging between extension contexts. WXT's [messaging guide](https://wxt.dev/guide/essentials/messaging) recommends this library over raw `browser.runtime.onMessage` because it handles `return true`, type safety, and error handling automatically.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/msg-use-ports-for-streams.md`: For long-lived connections or streaming data, use `runtime.connect` ports instead of repeated `sendMessage` calls. Ports maintain an open channel without reconnection overhead.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/msg-use-tabs-sendmessage.md`: Use `browser.tabs.sendMessage` to send messages from background to specific content scripts. Using `runtime.sendMessage` from background won't reach content scripts.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/store-batch-operations.md`: Multiple sequential storage operations trigger multiple disk writes. Group related data into a single `defineItem` to reduce I/O overhead.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/store-choose-storage-area.md`: WXT supports three storage areas with different characteristics. Choose based on persistence needs, sync requirements, and data size.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/store-handle-quota-errors.md`: Storage areas have quota limits. Always handle `QUOTA_BYTES_PER_ITEM` and `QUOTA_BYTES` errors to prevent data loss and provide user feedback.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/store-use-define-item.md`: Use `storage.defineItem` to define typed storage items with fallback values. This prevents null checks scattered throughout your code and catches type errors at build time.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/store-versioned-migrations.md`: Production extensions evolve over time. Use `version` and `migrations` in `storage.defineItem` to safely transform stored data when the schema changes.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/store-watch-for-changes.md`: Use `watch()` to react to storage changes instead of polling or manual refresh. This ensures all contexts (popup, content scripts, options) stay synchronized.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/svc-avoid-global-state.md`: Service workers can terminate after 30 seconds of inactivity. Any in-memory state is lost when this happens. Use persistent storage for all state that must survive restarts.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/svc-declarative-net-request.md`: Declarative Net Request rules execute in the browser's network stack, not in JavaScript. They're faster than webRequest and work even when the service worker is inactive.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/svc-handle-install-update.md`: The `runtime.onInstalled` event fires on first install, extension update, and browser update. Use it to initialize storage, run migrations, and set up default state.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/svc-keep-alive-patterns.md`: Service workers terminate after approximately 30 seconds of inactivity. For operations that take longer, use keep-alive patterns like periodic alarms or port connections.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/svc-offscreen-documents.md`: MV3 service workers cannot access DOM APIs. Use offscreen documents for operations requiring DOM like clipboard access, audio playback, or canvas manipulation.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/svc-register-listeners-synchronously.md`: Service workers can terminate at any time and restart when events occur. Event listeners must be registered synchronously at the top level of the service worker to ensure they are active before any events fire.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ts-augment-browser-types.md`: Some browser APIs aren't in the default type definitions. Augment the types instead of using `any` or `@ts-ignore`.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ts-avoid-any.md`: Message handlers using `any` miss type errors that cause silent runtime failures. Define explicit message types for compile-time safety.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ts-import-meta-env.md`: WXT exposes build information through `import.meta`. Use these instead of hardcoding values or relying on runtime detection.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ts-path-aliases.md`: Configure path aliases to avoid deep relative imports. This improves readability and makes moving files safer during refactoring.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ts-strict-null-checks.md`: Enable strict null checks in TypeScript to catch potential null reference errors. Extension APIs frequently return null for missing data.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ts-type-entrypoint-options.md`: Use explicit types for entrypoint configuration to catch typos and invalid options at build time instead of runtime.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ts-use-browser-not-chrome.md`: Use the `browser` namespace from WXT instead of `chrome`. WXT normalizes `chrome` to `browser` for cross-browser compatibility across Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and Edge.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ts-use-imports-module.md`: WXT v0.20+ provides `#imports` as a unified virtual module for all WXT APIs. This replaces scattered imports from `wxt/storage`, `wxt/sandbox`, `wxt/client`, etc. WXT also auto-imports common utilities, so explicit imports are often unnecessary.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ui-avoid-layout-thrashing.md`: Reading layout properties then writing causes forced synchronous reflows. Batch reads and writes separately to avoid layout thrashing on host pages.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ui-cleanup-on-unmount.md`: Content script UIs must clean up event listeners, observers, and timers when removed. Use `onRemove` for cleanup and `ctx` helpers for auto-cleanup.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ui-defer-rendering.md`: Popups re-render every time they open. Defer heavy initialization until the popup is actually displayed to minimize perceived latency.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ui-position-fixed-iframe.md`: For complex UIs injected into pages, use a positioned iframe instead of rendering directly. This provides complete isolation and allows using full frameworks without affecting page performance.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ui-sidepanel-persistence.md`: Unlike popups, sidepanels can persist state while open. Save state to storage on changes to survive sidepanel closes and browser restarts.
- `.claude/skills/wxt-browser-extensions/references/ui-use-shadow-dom.md`: When injecting UI into web pages from content scripts, use Shadow DOM to isolate your styles from the host page. This prevents both directions of style leakage.

